// The AI orchestration layer: the provider-agnostic tool-calling loop. The single seam between the
// loop and a concrete model API is ONE request — `infer_step` — so a provider is a module that serves
// it (`use <some provider>.provider(...)` at the app root) and nothing in this module is a decision
// any particular API forced. Adding a provider is adding a module; nothing here changes.
//
// NOTHING HERE NAMES A PROVIDER AS PART OF A SPECIFICATION, and that is a rule with teeth rather than a
// preference. A vocabulary element, an error arm or a policy justified by one API's behaviour is that
// API's wire model wearing the abstraction's clothes: the provider it came from then maps it with the
// identity function while every other provider translates, and the next provider inherits a shape
// chosen for someone else. So the vocabulary is closed Katari sums (`ai.types`), the tool-name
// reshaping a narrow wire vocabulary needs lives in the provider layer (`ai.wire_names`), and a failure
// only a provider can name rides `provider_error` rather than earning an arm here. A provider may
// still be MENTIONED as an example — "an API that keys tool results by name", "Anthropic's document
// block" — where the example makes the rule concrete; what may not happen is a rule whose only
// justification is that one provider works that way.
//
// Everything else is plain Katari over the agnostic vocabulary in `ai.types`: derive each
// tool's schema with `reflection.get_metadata`, ask the ambient provider for a step, dispatch tool
// calls concurrently with `parallel for` (each validated against its tool's schema by
// `reflection.call_agent` — an `as_tool` tool from an MCP server validates against the server's
// schema the same way), and feed the results back until the model answers.
//
// Files flow through the loop as blob handles: a tool result's `file` values are collected off the
// result's wire form (`collect_result_files`) and ride the `tool_result`, so the provider can inline
// their content for the model while the result is fresh — and the model can replay any handle it has
// seen into a later tool call (`view_file` re-reads one on demand). WHICH content a provider can inline
// is one decision over the file's recorded content type — the `file_kind` sum below, which every
// provider dispatches over.
//
// THE THREE LAYERS THIS PACKAGE OFFERS, smallest first: `infer_step` (the seam) and `take_turn` (one
// model turn as a value) for a hand-rolled loop; `serve_session` / `serve_observations` for a single
// conversation served behind one request; and the DESK — a keyed, resident conversation whose state is
// data this package owns — for a program with more than one of them. See the desk section below.

import ai.types

@"One model step over the conversation + tool schemas — THE provider seam. A provider module serves
this request for the extent of a continuation (`use <some provider>.provider(...)`), so the loop stays
provider-agnostic and providers stay loop-agnostic. The reply is an OUTCOME sum, never a throw: on
success `inferred(result)` carries the step's decision AND its token accounting (`types.step_result`,
so any loop over this seam can meter its context for free); on failure `inference_failed(error)`
carries the `step_error` the provider gave up on (its retries exhausted, or a fatal status). Handing the
failure back as a VALUE — rather than throwing from the provider's DEEP handler — is what lets each loop
decide at its own PERFORM site whether to absorb the failure (the region loop abandons just that turn and
lives on) or re-raise it into its own caller (the one-shot loops): a deep handler can only throw OUT of
its own context, never INTO the loop's, so the resume value is the only channel that reaches the loop."
request infer_step(history: array[types.message], tool_metas: array[reflection.agent_metadata]) -> step_outcome

// The error surface of one model step. Three arms describe a REQUEST/REPLY transport, because that is
// what a hosted model API is and what every classification above this line wants to read: a non-2xx
// status (`http.status_error` — the arm an app dispatches on to tell "check your credit balance" from
// "wait it out"), a transport failure (`http.fetch_error`), and a malformed reply (`json.parse_error`).
// The FOURTH arm is what keeps those three from being a specification: `provider_error` carries a
// failure only the provider can name — a reply that finished with no answer, a refusal an SDK reports
// out of band, a local model's own error — as the provider's own value, so a provider whose failures
// are not HTTP-shaped has somewhere to put them and no provider has to invent an arm here.
// A provider no longer THROWS this — it hands it back as `inference_failed(error)` (see `infer_step`),
// so the failure surfaces at the loop's perform site, not the provider's deep use site. (Type
// synonyms take no docs.)
type step_error = json.parse_error | http.status_error | http.fetch_error | provider_error

@"A step failure only the PROVIDER can name — the arm that keeps `step_error` from being a list of
transport shapes and lets a provider carry its own failure as a value. @source@ says who produced it
(a provider module's name, or a middleware's), @message@ is the one human-readable line an app can
show, @transient@ is the PROVIDER's own verdict on whether trying again could help (`is_transient`
reads it rather than guessing about a failure it has never seen), and @detail@ carries the provider's
own typed datum — so an app that knows the provider can `match` it out, and one that does not still
has a message and a verdict."
data provider_error(
  @"Who produced this failure — the provider module or middleware that built it." source: string,
  @"One human-readable line." message: string,
  @"The producer's own verdict: could a retry help?" transient: boolean,
  @"The producer's own typed datum, for an app that wants to match on it." detail: unknown,
)

@"One model step's OUTCOME success: the provider produced a step (`types.step_result` for `infer_step`,
`types.object_result` for `infer_object`). Generic over the payload so both seam requests share one sum shape."
data inferred[T](@"The step the provider produced." result: T)

@"One model step's OUTCOME failure: the provider gave up — a transient error past its retry budget, or a
fatal status / malformed body. Carried back as a VALUE so a loop handles it at its perform site, rather than
the provider throwing out of its own deep handler and unwinding the whole loop with it."
data inference_failed(@"The step error the provider surfaced." error: step_error)

// The outcome of one `infer_step`: the produced step, or the provider's surfaced failure. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type step_outcome = inferred[types.step_result] | inference_failed

// The outcome of one `infer_object`: the produced object, or the provider's surfaced failure. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type object_outcome = inferred[types.object_result] | inference_failed

@"One structured-output step over the conversation — the seam's second request, served by the same
provider handler as `infer_step`. @schema@ is the JSON Schema the reply must conform to (each
provider maps it onto its native constrained decoding); the reply is an OUTCOME — `inferred(result)`
with the decoded object and usage, or `inference_failed(error)`. Prefer `infer_structured[T]`, which
reifies T's schema, validates the reply, and re-raises a failure at your own call site."
request infer_object(history: array[types.message], schema: unknown) -> object_outcome

@"Infer a single reply with no tools (the common chat case). A provider failure re-raises here as
`prelude.throw[step_error]` — this is a one-shot call, so a caller that wants to survive a failed step
installs its own handler (the region loop does not use this; it drives the seam directly and absorbs)."
agent reply(history: array[types.message]) -> string {
  match (infer_step(history = history, tool_metas = [])) {
    case inferred(result => result) -> {
      match (result.step) {
        case types.step_final(text => text) -> text
        case types.step_call(calls => _) -> "(unexpected tool call without tools)"
      }
    }
    case inference_failed(error => error) -> prelude.throw(error = error)
  }
}

@"Infer a single reply as a typed value — structured output with the type as the contract:
`reflection.schema_of[T]` reifies T's schema, the ambient provider decodes against it natively
(a response schema, a response format, a forced tool — whichever its API offers), and the decoded object
validates as T (`json.validate` — a nonconforming reply throws `json.validation_error`). Instantiate
T explicitly; it appears only in the result, so it is never inferable. An object type
(`type shape = { ... }`) makes the friendliest model-facing schema — a data type asks the model to
produce its `$katari_constructor` wrapper too."
agent infer_structured[T](history: array[types.message]) -> T {
  match (infer_object(history = history, schema = reflection.schema_of[T]())) {
    case inferred(result => result) -> json.validate[T](value = result.value)
    case inference_failed(error => error) -> prelude.throw(error = error)
  }
}

// ── transient retry: the provider layer's resilience ──────────────────────────
//
// A provider's `infer_step` / `infer_object` handler runs at the app root (its `use` site), ABOVE the
// loop — so a step failure (`step_error`) surfaces THERE and the tool loop cannot catch it (the loop is the
// handler's continuation, not an enclosing handler). A transient failure — a 429 rate limit, a 5xx, a
// dropped connection — would therefore end a long-lived bot. The fix lives where the error IS catchable:
// inside the provider's own handler body, whose effect row is CONCRETE, so a `prelude.throw[step_error]`
// handler discharges it (the same shape as an app's resilient `try_send`). Each provider retries a
// transient step with exponential backoff; these two pure helpers make the decision, and the retry loop
// itself lives in each handler body — a callback-wrapped `retry(task)` would commonise the whole thing,
// which is not the point (the decision is the shared part, not the control flow).

@"Whether a step error is TRANSIENT — worth a backoff and a retry — as opposed to fatal. A transport
failure (`http.fetch_error`: DNS, a refused connection, a timeout, a mid-flight restart) is always
transient; a status error is transient for 408 (request timeout), 429 (rate limit) and any 5xx
(server-side), and fatal for every other status (401/403/404 … — a retry will not fix them); a
`json.parse_error` is fatal (a malformed body will not parse on a retry). A `provider_error` answers
with the verdict its PRODUCER wrote into it: this function cannot classify a failure it has never
seen, and guessing on the producer's behalf is how a deterministic failure earns a retry ladder."
agent is_transient(error: step_error) -> boolean {
  match (error) {
    case http.fetch_error(message => _) -> true
    case http.status_error(status => status, body => _) -> status == 408 || status == 429 || status >= 500
    case json.parse_error(message => _) -> false
    case provider_error(source => _, message => _, transient => transient, detail => _) -> transient
  }
}

@"The backoff delay in milliseconds before retry @attempt@ (0-based): @base_ms@ doubled per attempt (base,
2×base, 4×base, …) and capped at one minute, so a long outage does not sleep unboundedly between the final
tries."
agent backoff_ms(attempt: integer, base_ms: number) -> number {
  let grown = for (let _step in array.range(start = 0, end = attempt), var delay: number = base_ms) {
    next with { delay = delay * 2.0 }
  } then (_steps) { delay }
  math.min(left = grown, right = 60000.0)
}

@"The outcome of ONE provider step attempt, threaded back to a provider's retry loop: the step's @result@
on success. A provider's per-attempt helper catches a `step_error` into `attempt_err` so the loop can
classify it (`is_transient`) and either back off and retry or propagate."
data attempt_ok[T](result: T)

@"The outcome of ONE provider step attempt: the @error@ the attempt caught, for the retry loop to classify."
data attempt_err(error: step_error)

// A step attempt's verdict: the result, or the caught error to classify and maybe retry. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type attempt[T] = attempt_ok[T] | attempt_err

@"Two tools in one loop's tool list share a metadata name, so the model's pick could not be routed
unambiguously (dispatch matches by name — the first match would silently win, and the other tool
would be unreachable). A configuration defect the app fixes — a `prefix` on one of the colliding MCP
connections, or a rename — typed so a supervisor reports it instead of the run panicking."
data duplicate_tool(@"The name two tools share." name: string)

// THE ONE THING THIS LAYER KNOWS ABOUT TOOL NAMES, and the seam it leaves the providers. A tool's
// derived name is a qualified Katari name and the loop dispatches by matching it, so the only property
// the LOOP needs is that the names of one step are distinct (`ensure_unique_names`). Whether a name is
// SPELLABLE on the wire is a different question and not this layer's: an API may validate tool names
// against an alphabet that forbids the qualified name's dot, or cap their length. A provider whose
// vocabulary is too narrow reshapes the name on its own wire and reverses the model's pick before
// handing the call back (`ai.wire_names` holds that machinery for the providers that share it) — the
// contract in this direction is one line: whatever a provider sends, `tool_call.name` must come back
// as the REAL qualified name, because that is what dispatch matches.

@"Validate one step's assembled tool metadata: every name unique, or the typed `duplicate_tool`.
Every loop runs its tool list through this once per turn, so a colliding list fails loudly at the
first step instead of silently routing every pick to whichever tool sorts first."
agent ensure_unique_names(metas: array[reflection.agent_metadata]) -> array[reflection.agent_metadata] {
  for (let meta in metas, var seen: record[boolean] = record.empty()) {
    if (record.has(target = seen, key = meta.name)) {
      prelude.throw(error = duplicate_tool(name = meta.name))
    } else {
      next with { seen = record.set(target = seen, key = meta.name, value = true) }
    }
  } then (_metas) { null }
  metas
}

// ── attached files: one dispatch over the recorded content type ───────────────
//
// How a file reaches a model follows from ONE datum: its recorded content type. `classify_file` turns
// that string into the closed `file_kind` sum, and each provider MATCHES the sum — one arm per wire
// shape its own API accepts (Anthropic's `document` block, Gemini's `inlineData` part, a text block, or
// the note that says plainly the content cannot be read). The classification lives here, once, so the
// providers cannot drift on what counts as textual; the arms live in the providers, because only a
// provider knows its own wire.
//
// The content type IS the data and it is taken as recorded: nothing here reads a file's bytes to guess a
// better type. A sender that labels a .md as `application/octet-stream` therefore lands in
// `opaque_file`, and the model is told it cannot read it — the fix belongs at the upload boundary that
// recorded the type, not in a sniffer here that would sometimes be wrong about a file the model then
// answers from.

@"An image the model can look at (`image/*`). @content_type@ is the exact recorded type because that is
what the wire's `media_type` / `mimeType` field must carry — the family decided the arm, the exact type
rides along as its datum."
data image_file(@"The file's recorded content type." content_type: string)

@"A PDF (`application/pdf`): a document a model reads as pages — text, tables and figures alike — on a
provider whose API has a document shape for it (Anthropic's `document` block, Gemini's `inlineData`)."
data document_file(@"The file's recorded content type." content_type: string)

@"A file whose bytes ARE text: any `text/*` (plain, markdown, csv, html, …), plus `application/json`,
`application/xml` and `application/csv`. This arm needs no API support at all — the decoded text inlines
as ordinary text (capped; see `file_text_note`), so every provider serves it."
data text_file(@"The file's recorded content type." content_type: string)

@"Audio (`audio/*`): inlinable on a provider whose model takes audio input, a note to the model on one
that does not."
data audio_file(@"The file's recorded content type." content_type: string)

@"Video (`video/*`): inlinable on a provider whose model takes video input, a note to the model on one
that does not."
data video_file(@"The file's recorded content type." content_type: string)

@"A file no arm above covers — an archive, an office document, a font, an unrecorded or gone handle.
No provider can put its content in front of the model, so every provider reports it as unreadable
instead of shipping a bare handle the model would answer from."
data opaque_file(@"The file's recorded content type; \"\" when none was recorded." content_type: string)

// How ONE attached file can reach a model, off its recorded content type. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type file_kind = image_file | document_file | text_file | audio_file | video_file | opaque_file

@"The file's recorded content type, or \"\" when the handle is gone (freed, or an id that never named a
live blob). A gone file has no content to inline — the model is told so — so `gone` folds to the same
unreadable signal as an unrecorded type and never fails a whole step over one stale attachment."
agent file_content_type(@"The file to read." attachment: file) -> string {
  // `files.content_type` throws `files.gone` for a stale handle. Catch exactly that and recover with ""
  // (an unrecorded type, hence `opaque_file`), so one stale attachment never fails a whole step.
  use handler {
    request prelude.throw(error: files.gone) -> never { break "" }
  }
  files.content_type(value = attachment)
}

@"@content_type@ in the one form both a dispatch and a wire field can use: the media type alone,
lowercased, with any parameter dropped (\"Text/Plain; charset=utf-8\" becomes \"text/plain\"), and a
confirmed alias resolved to the registered type (`canonical_content_type`). RFC 2045 makes a media type
case-insensitive and lets a sender append parameters, and an HTTP download records exactly those forms — so
the raw string is the wrong thing to compare (`application/pdf; version=1.7` would miss the PDF arm and read
as unreadable) and the wrong thing to ship (a provider's `media_type` field takes the bare type)."
agent normalized_content_type(@"The recorded content type." content_type: string) -> string {
  let without_parameters = match (string.index_of(value = content_type, search = ";")) {
    case null -> content_type
    case index -> string.slice(value = content_type, start = 0, end = index)
  }
  canonical_content_type(content_type = string.to_lower(value = string.trim(value = without_parameters)))
}

@"An ALIAS resolved to the registered media type for the same format — the second half of normalization,
after case and parameters. A sender that derived its label from a file extension writes `image/jpg` for the
JPEG it is sending, and the accepted-type lists (the providers' and IANA's alike) name only `image/jpeg`:
without this the file takes the unreadable note although the API would have read it happily. Resolving the
alias is strictly better than either alternative — it inlines, and nothing off-list is ever sent.
Only labels CONFIRMED to name the identical format belong here. An unconfirmed guess turns a recoverable
note into a 400, which is the trade this whole dispatch exists to avoid; deliberately absent, for that
reason, are `image/pjpeg` (an IE-era label MDN's image-types guide does not equate to `image/jpeg`),
`audio/mpeg` (RFC 3003 covers MPEG-1/2 layers I–III, so it is not necessarily the MP3 that Gemini's
`audio/mp3` names) and `application/x-pdf` (no registry or vendor document equates it)."
agent canonical_content_type(@"The lowercased, parameter-free content type." content_type: string) -> string {
  match (content_type) {
    // IANA registers `image/jpeg` ("jpeg,image/jpeg,[RFC2045][RFC2046]") and no `image/jpg`; MDN's
    // image-types guide lists `.jpg` among JPEG's own extensions, so an `image/jpg` label is a JPEG.
    case "image/jpg" -> "image/jpeg"
    // IANA registers `video/quicktime` ("quicktime,video/quicktime,[RFC6381][Paul_Lindner]") and no
    // `video/mov`; `.mov` IS the QuickTime container, so the two labels name one format.
    case "video/mov" -> "video/quicktime"
    case recorded -> recorded
  }
}

@"The content type's FAMILY — the token before the \"/\" (\"image\" for \"image/png\"), or the whole
string when there is no slash. The family is the dispatch key: every `image/*` reaches a model the same
way, so a provider needs one arm per family rather than one per media type."
agent content_type_family(@"The recorded content type." content_type: string) -> string {
  match (string.index_of(value = content_type, search = "/")) {
    case null -> content_type
    case index -> string.slice(value = content_type, start = 0, end = index)
  }
}

@"How @attachment@ can reach a model, from its recorded content type alone — the `file_kind` sum every
provider dispatches over. The `application/*` types that are not their own family are named exactly (a
PDF is a document; JSON / XML / CSV are text), and everything else falls to its family. The type the
verdict carries is the NORMALIZED one, so a provider ships a `media_type` its API accepts whatever form
the sender recorded."
agent classify_file(@"The file to classify." attachment: file) -> file_kind {
  let content_type = normalized_content_type(content_type = file_content_type(attachment = attachment))
  match (content_type) {
    case "application/pdf" -> document_file(content_type = content_type)
    case "application/json" -> text_file(content_type = content_type)
    case "application/xml" -> text_file(content_type = content_type)
    case "application/csv" -> text_file(content_type = content_type)
    case _ -> classify_content_type_family(content_type = content_type)
  }
}

@"The family half of `classify_file`: `image/*`, `text/*`, `audio/*`, `video/*` — and `opaque_file` for
any other family, an unrecorded type included."
agent classify_content_type_family(@"The recorded content type." content_type: string) -> file_kind {
  match (content_type_family(content_type = content_type)) {
    case "image" -> image_file(content_type = content_type)
    case "text" -> text_file(content_type = content_type)
    case "audio" -> audio_file(content_type = content_type)
    case "video" -> video_file(content_type = content_type)
    case _ -> opaque_file(content_type = content_type)
  }
}

// The notes below name no OTHER package's tool, on purpose. This package cannot know which tools the app
// handed the model, and a note promising one the holder lacks sends the model chasing a tool that is not
// there — which is exactly how `send_files` stayed advertised here long after the chat packages deleted
// it. State what the handle IS and let the model match it against the tool list it actually holds;
// `view_file` is the one tool this package owns, so it is the one a note may name.

@"A file as its `$katari_ref` handle note — the resting representation every provider shows for an
attachment. The handle is the exact object a model replays into a tool call, so the note IS the
affordance and not a description: any tool the model holds that takes a file accepts this value."
agent file_handle_note(@"The file to note." attachment: file) -> string {
  f"[attached file: ${json.stringify(value = attachment)}]"
}

@"How a content type reads inside a note: the type itself, or the statement that none was recorded (a
bare \"\" tells a model nothing)."
agent content_type_label(@"The recorded content type." content_type: string) -> string {
  match (content_type) {
    case "" -> "no recorded content type"
    case recorded -> f"content type ${recorded}"
  }
}

@"The note for a file this model connection cannot show: the handle, the content type, and the plain
statement that the CONTENT is absent. Stating the limit is the whole point — handed a bare handle with
no content and no explanation, a model answers from the file's look and invents the rest, so the note
tells it to report the gap instead."
agent unreadable_file_note(
  @"The file to note." attachment: file,
  @"Its recorded content type." content_type: string,
) -> string {
  f"[attached file: ${json.stringify(value = attachment)} — ${content_type_label(content_type = content_type)}. Its CONTENT is not available to you: this model connection cannot read that content type. Say so if you are asked about it; do not guess what it contains.]"
}

@"A textual file as text the model reads: a header naming the handle and the content type, then the
decoded content, cut at @max_characters@ code points with an explicit marker when it overflows. The
marker is not decoration — a model that cannot tell it is holding a FRAGMENT answers confidently from
the fragment. The 24000-code-point default is ~6k tokens: half again the `web` package's page budget,
because an attachment cannot be re-read at another offset the way a page can be re-fetched, and still
small enough that several attachments fit one turn."
agent file_text_note(
  @"The file to inline." attachment: file,
  @"Its recorded content type." content_type: string,
  @"The truncation budget, in code points." max_characters: integer ?= 24000,
) -> string {
  let text = file_text(attachment = attachment)
  let length = string.length(value = text)
  let header = f"[attached file: ${json.stringify(value = attachment)} — ${content_type_label(content_type = content_type)}, ${length} characters. Its text follows.]"
  if (length > max_characters) {
    let head = string.slice(value = text, start = 0, end = max_characters)
    f"${header}\n${head}\n[… TRUNCATED: the first ${max_characters} of ${length} characters are shown; the rest was not sent. Treat this as a fragment, and say so rather than answering as if you had the whole file.]"
  } else {
    f"${header}\n${text}"
  }
}

@"A file's bytes decoded as UTF-8 text — the read behind `file_text_note`. The whole content transits the
value plane because the runtime has no partial-read primitive; it is a PRIM read, recomputed on replay
rather than recorded, so nothing durable grows by the file's size. A gone handle reads as \"\", the same
recovery as `file_content_type` (classification already routes a gone handle to `opaque_file`, so this is
totality for the vanishing case of a file freed between the two reads)."
agent file_text(@"The file to read." attachment: file) -> string {
  use handler {
    request prelude.throw(error: files.gone) -> never { break "" }
  }
  string.from_base64(value = files.read_base64(value = attachment))
}

// ── the inline window: how far back a file's CONTENT is re-sent ────────────────
//
// Every step re-sends the whole conversation, so a file that inlines forever is uploaded again on every
// step: a 10 MB PDF in a thirty-step tool loop is 300 MB of request bodies and thirty upload waits, which a
// human watching a chat channel feels as latency. Prompt caching does not help — it saves the model's
// COMPUTE on a prefix it has seen, not the client's bytes on the wire.
//
// So only the newest @keep@ FILE-BEARING history elements inline their content; older files show their
// handle note alone, and the model re-reads any of them on demand with `view_file`. That recovery path is
// what makes the degradation honest, and it holds wherever a tool result's files reach the model at all,
// which is what it took for this policy to be safe.
//
// KEYED TO FILES, NOT TO POSITION, and that is the whole design. It follows from a property every model
// API shares, whatever it calls it: a repeated request prefix is only reused — cached, discounted,
// however the API words it — while it stays BYTE-IDENTICAL. (Some APIs do it implicitly, some ask the
// client to mark where the prefix ends; the requirement is the same either way.) A positional window
// ("the last element inlines") slides on EVERY step: each tool call appends an element, the element that
// inlined a moment ago degrades to a note, and the rendered prefix changes underneath a conversation
// that did not gain a single file — so a tool loop re-sends a changed prefix on every step and no prefix
// reuse can survive it. Keyed to files, the window moves only when a NEW file arrives; an element
// without files renders identically inlined or not, so appending tool calls, tool results and model
// turns leaves every earlier byte alone. A new attachment does rewrite the tail, at a turn boundary the
// app was paying for anyway.

@"How many files a history element carries — a turn's attachments, or every file across a result batch.
The count is what makes an element FILE-BEARING; a call turn never carries one."
agent message_file_count(@"The history element to weigh." message: types.message) -> integer {
  match (message) {
    case types.turn(role => _, text => _, files => files, author => _) -> array.length(target = files)
    case types.call_turn(calls => _) -> 0
    case types.result_turn(results => results) -> {
      for (let result in results, var total: integer = 0) {
        next with { total = total + array.length(target = result.files) }
      } then (_results) { total }
    }
  }
}

@"The index of the OLDEST history element whose files still inline: the newest @keep@ FILE-BEARING elements
inline their content, and everything before this index shows handle notes only. A history with no file at
all answers its own length — there is nothing to inline, and the split is empty either way.
Providers render `slice(0, index)` with inlining off and `slice(index, length)` with it on, so the whole
policy is one integer and each provider keeps its own rendering."
agent inline_from_index(
  @"The conversation so far." history: array[types.message],
  @"How many of the newest file-bearing elements keep their content inline." keep: integer ?= 1,
) -> integer {
  let bearing = for (let message in history, var count: integer = 0) {
    if (message_file_count(message = message) == 0) {
      next with { count = count }
    } else {
      next with { count = count + 1 }
    }
  } then (_messages) { count }
  // One flag per element: an element inlines when it carries files AND is among the newest @keep@ of the
  // file-bearing ones (`seen` counts those before it, so this one is the `seen + 1`-th).
  let flags = for (let message in history, var seen: integer = 0) {
    if (message_file_count(message = message) == 0) {
      next false with { seen = seen }
    } else {
      next bearing - seen <= keep with { seen = seen + 1 }
    }
  } then (marks) { marks }
  // The FIRST inlining element is the boundary; the elements after it that carry no file render the same
  // either way, so one index describes the window exactly.
  match (array.index_of(target = flags, value = true)) {
    case null -> array.length(target = history)
    case index -> index
  }
}

@"Tool: read a file that is in this conversation — an image, a PDF, or a text document. Pass the file's
handle object; a bare {\"$katari_ref\": \"<id>\"} suffices (copy the id from the conversation).
A text document (`text/*`, JSON, XML, CSV) comes back AS TEXT in this tool's result, cut at a cap with an
explicit truncation marker when it is long. An image, a PDF, audio or video comes back as the file itself,
which the model connection places in your context if its API accepts that type — so if the content does
not appear, say you cannot see it rather than guessing. A content type nothing can read (an archive, an
office document, an unrecorded type) comes back as a note saying exactly that, and reporting it is then
the correct answer."
agent view_file(@"The file to read: its handle object, as it appears in the conversation." attachment: file) -> file | string {
  // The same content-type dispatch the providers run, at the one point where an arm can answer with a VALUE
  // instead of a wire block — which is why two arms answer here rather than deferring: text needs no API
  // support, so it reads identically on every provider, and an unreadable type is unreadable on all of them,
  // so saying so beats handing back a bare handle the model would answer from. Every other kind returns the
  // file for the provider to inline through whichever block its own API accepts.
  match (classify_file(attachment = attachment)) {
    case text_file(content_type => content_type) -> file_text_note(attachment = attachment, content_type = content_type)
    case opaque_file(content_type => content_type) -> unreadable_file_note(attachment = attachment, content_type = content_type)
    case _ -> attachment
  }
}

@"Run the tool-using model loop, generic over the tools' shared effect @E@: derive each tool's schema
with `reflection.get_metadata`, then up to @max_steps@ times perform `infer_step` against the ambient
provider; on a batch of tool calls, dispatch them all CONCURRENTLY with `parallel for` (each resolves
its tool by name and invokes it by value via `reflection.call_agent`, validating the model's args
against the tool's schema), then feed every result back as a new turn; on a final answer, return it."
agent infer_with_tools[effect E lacks time.race_settled](
  history: array[types.message],
  tools: array[agent never -> unknown with {...E, prelude.throw[unknown]}],
  max_steps: integer,
  @"The per-tool-call budget in milliseconds, or `null` (the default) for no deadline — see `take_turn`." tool_budget_milliseconds: number | null ?= null,
  @"Tool names exempt from the deadline race (human-latency tools) — see `take_turn`." unbounded_tool_names: array[string] ?= [],
) -> string {
  // The one turn loop lives in `take_turn`; this one-shot shape re-raises a failure at the perform
  // site (a one-shot caller has no loop to absorb into) and keeps only the reply.
  match (take_turn[E](conversation = history, tools = tools, max_steps = max_steps, tool_budget_milliseconds = tool_budget_milliseconds, unbounded_tool_names = unbounded_tool_names)) {
    case session_turn(reply => reply_text, conversation => _, used_tokens => _, tool_events => _) -> reply_text
    case failed_turn(conversation => _, error => error, tool_events => _) -> prelude.throw(error = error)
  }
}

@"WHO a step is on behalf of: the @author@ of the NEWEST user turn in @history@ — taken AS-IS, null
included, with no further scanning back. The newest user turn is the turn that triggered the current
model turn, so a system-triggered turn (a monitor tick, whose injection carries `author = null`)
shields the earlier human turns instead of misattributing the step to whoever spoke last; the
loop-synthesized user turns (the budget close, the empty-final renudge) are also null and likewise
read as unattributed. A provider whose API has an end-user metadata field (`metadata.user_id`,
`safety_identifier`, whatever it is called there) lifts a non-null result into it; one that has no
such field ignores it."
agent attribution(history: array[types.message]) -> string | null {
  for (let message in history, var newest: string | null = null) {
    match (message) {
      case types.turn(role => role, text => _, files => _, author => author) -> {
        // A LATER user turn always overwrites — a null author on the newest user turn deliberately
        // erases an earlier human's tag rather than letting the scan reach past the trigger.
        match (role) {
          case types.user_role(_) -> { next with { newest = author } }
          case types.model_role(_) -> { next with { newest = newest } }
        }
      }
      case _ -> { next with { newest = newest } }
    }
  } then (_messages) { newest }
}

@"The one-shot renudge a loop plays when the model's final step came back EMPTY: an empty string is
a lost answer in an interactive exchange (a thinking model can finish a heavy turn with only thought
parts and no text), so the loop asks ONCE for a plain-text answer before accepting the silence. The
wording also names the legitimate way out — a watcher protocol with truly nothing to report answers
empty again, and the second empty is accepted as-is."
agent empty_reply_nudge() -> types.message {
  types.turn(
    role = types.user_role(),
    text = "(Your previous reply was empty. If you have an answer, state it now in plain text. If you truly have nothing to say or report, reply with an empty message once more.)",
    files = [],
  )
}

@"The forced tool-less close every loop plays when its step budget runs out: a user turn telling the
model the budget is spent, so ONE last step commits to an answer from what the tools already gathered
instead of discarding it."
agent budget_exhausted_turn() -> types.message {
  types.turn(
    role = types.user_role(),
    text = "You have used your entire tool-call budget. Do not call any more tools. Answer the original question now, as best you can, using only what you have already gathered.",
    files = [],
  )
}

// ── tool events: what the loop swallowed, said out loud ───────────────────────
//
// A failing tool must never fail the conversation, so `dispatch_one` folds EVERY failure into a result
// the model reads and corrects from. That is right for the model and blind for the app: a tool that
// panics on every call, an MCP connection whose every invocation times out, a name the model keeps
// hallucinating — all of it used to be visible only to the model, in prose, inside a conversation the
// app does not read. So each dispatch also yields one EVENT, and a turn carries the batch of them out
// (`session_turn.tool_events`). Nothing in the loop branches on an event: it is a report, and what to
// do with it — log it, count it, alert on it — is the app's.

@"One tool call went through: the tool ran and returned."
data tool_succeeded()

@"One tool call FAILED and the model was told so: the tool panicked, or threw its own typed error, or
the delegation boundary refused the model's arguments. @message@ is the same line the model reads."
data tool_failed(@"The failure, as the model was told it." message: string)

@"One tool call outran its deadline and was cancelled — the loop told the model, and this says so out
loud too, because a tool that always times out is an app-level fault and not a model-level one."
data tool_timed_out(@"The budget it outran, in milliseconds." budget_milliseconds: number)

@"The model picked a name no tool in the list carries — a hallucinated tool, or one the app forgot to
hand out. The model is told; a run of these is worth an app's attention."
data tool_not_found()

// How ONE dispatched tool call ended. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type tool_outcome = tool_succeeded | tool_failed | tool_timed_out | tool_not_found

@"ONE tool call of a turn, as the app can observe it: which tool the model picked and how the call
ended. The loop never branches on this — it is the report of what the loop already handled."
data tool_event(@"The tool name the model picked." name: string, @"How the call ended." outcome: tool_outcome)

@"One dispatched tool call: the result fed back to the model, and the event reported to the app. They
are returned together because they are two readings of ONE call and must not be able to disagree."
data dispatched(@"What the model is told." result: types.tool_result, @"What the app is told." event: tool_event)

@"Find the tool whose metadata name is the model's pick and invoke it by value via
`reflection.call_agent` (the model's args ride as a plain value, and the delegation boundary
validates them against the tool's schema — the compiled signature of a Katari agent, or the
server-declared schema of an MCP tool — while the boundary's unconditional wire→value conversion turns
any replayed `$katari_ref` into the `file` it denotes). Every way a call can go wrong is fed back to the
model as an error result it reads and corrects on its next step: a hallucinated name (nothing matches), a
tool that panics, a tool's own typed `throw`, malformed args (a `reflection.call_error` from the
boundary), and a call that outruns @budget_milliseconds@ (raced via `time.with_deadline`, the late
call cancelled) alike — none of them reaches the app root. Each of those also yields a `tool_event`, so
what the model was told is not the ONLY record of it. The result's `file` values are collected off the
result tree so the provider can show them to the model (their `$katari_ref` handles are already visible in
the result text)."
agent dispatch_one[effect E lacks time.race_settled](tools: array[agent never -> unknown with {...E, prelude.throw[unknown]}], call: types.tool_call, @"The per-call budget in milliseconds, or `null` for no deadline." budget_milliseconds: number | null, @"Metadata names exempt from the deadline race — human-latency tools; see `take_turn`." unbounded_tool_names: array[string] ?= []) -> dispatched {
  // A failing tool must never fail the conversation — that is the loop's whole premise, and it holds for
  // BOTH ways a call can crash: a `panic` (a crashed handler) and any typed `throw` — the tool's own
  // `throw[my_error]` and the boundary's `throw[call_error]` for hallucinated args alike — fold back HERE
  // as an error result the model reads and corrects on its next step, instead of escalating to the app
  // root. This is why the tools' effect is spelled `{...E, prelude.throw[unknown]}`: `E` stays throw-free,
  // every error a tool can raise rides the EXPLICIT `prelude.throw[unknown]`, and the `throw(error: unknown)`
  // handler below discharges exactly that row — so `E` escalates untouched while every tool failure returns
  // to the model as feedback it can act on (a bad-arguments hallucination becomes self-correcting too).
  // The failure shape, spelled once for both catching handlers and the two miss cases: the model reads
  // @text@, the app reads @outcome@.
  agent refused(text: string, outcome: tool_outcome) -> dispatched {
    dispatched(
      result = types.tool_result(name = call.name, ref = call.ref, text = text, files = []),
      event = tool_event(name = call.name, outcome = outcome),
    )
  }
  use handler {
    request panic(msg: string) {
      break refused(text = f"(tool failed: ${msg})", outcome = tool_failed(message = msg))
    }
  }
  use handler {
    request prelude.throw(error: unknown) -> never {
      let rendered = json.stringify(value = error)
      break refused(text = f"(tool failed: ${rendered})", outcome = tool_failed(message = rendered))
    }
  }
  // The result a completed call folds to — shared by the bare and the deadlined path. `stringify`
  // renders the result's wire form (files show as `$katari_ref` handles) for the model, while the
  // actual `file` values are collected off the result tree for inlining.
  agent delivered(result: unknown) -> dispatched {
    dispatched(
      result = types.tool_result(
        name = call.name,
        ref = call.ref,
        text = json.stringify(value = result),
        files = collect_result_files(node = result),
      ),
      event = tool_event(name = call.name, outcome = tool_succeeded()),
    )
  }
  for (let tool in tools) {
    let meta = reflection.get_metadata(value = tool)
    if (meta.name == call.name) {
      // The args are already a plain value; the boundary unconditionally turns any replayed `$katari_ref`
      // into a `file`. A `null` budget calls the tool bare; a number races it against the deadline
      // (`time.with_deadline` — first wins, the loser cancelled), so no single tool can hang the turn:
      // the timeout comes back to the model as an error result it reads, like every other tool failure.
      // A name in @unbounded_tool_names@ is exempted the same way a null budget is: deadlines bound
      // MACHINE latency, and a tool legitimately waiting on a human is visible in the admin surface
      // while it waits, so it is left unbounded instead of cancelled mid-ask.
      let effective_budget = if (array.contains(target = unbounded_tool_names, value = call.name)) {
        null
      } else {
        budget_milliseconds
      }
      match (effective_budget) {
        case null -> {
          return delivered(result = reflection.call_agent(target = tool, args = call.args))
        }
        case limit -> {
          agent attempt(value: null) -> unknown with {...E, prelude.throw[unknown]} {
            reflection.call_agent(target = tool, args = call.args)
          }
          match (time.with_deadline(milliseconds = limit, task = attempt)) {
            case time.completed(value => result) -> { return delivered(result = result) }
            case time.deadline_passed() -> {
              return refused(
                text = f"(tool timed out after ${limit}ms; the call was cancelled)",
                outcome = tool_timed_out(budget_milliseconds = limit),
              )
            }
          }
        }
      }
    } else {
      null
    }
  }
  refused(text = f"(error: unknown tool \"${call.name}\")", outcome = tool_not_found())
}

@"Collect every `file` value out of a tool result's value tree: `reflection.call_agent` returns the
tool's result as a plain value, so a `file` the tool produced (a generated image, an MCP tool's
screenshot) is an actual `file` leaf — found wherever it sits (a bare return, the MCP bridge's
{ text, files } record, or nested structure)."
agent collect_result_files(node: unknown) -> array[file] with pure {
  match (node) {
    case file(found) -> [found]
    case record(entries) -> {
      array.flatten(target = for (let value in record.values(target = entries)) {
        next collect_result_files(node = value)
      })
    }
    case array(items) -> {
      array.flatten(target = for (let item in items) {
        next collect_result_files(node = item)
      })
    }
    case _ -> []
  }
}

// ── served sessions: the serve skeleton, with token accounting and compaction ─
//
// Every chat bot hand-rolled the same skeleton: declare a request, keep the conversation in a
// handler var, run the tool loop once per message, append the reply. `serve_session` is that
// skeleton as a scoped provider — `use serve_session(...)` installs the handler, the continuation
// bridges its transport into `session_message` and (typically) never returns, a watch loop — plus
// the piece none of the hand-rolled ones had: the seam's `usage` metered into a context-occupancy
// figure, and a compaction pass when it outgrows a threshold. The policy is data (`compaction`),
// the one numeric comparison lives in `classify_budget`, and everything downstream dispatches on
// its sum. Transport specifics (a channel id, a thread ts) stay in the app's bridge agent: fold
// them into @content@ on the way in, route the returned reply on the way out.

@"How a session compacts its context once it outgrows the threshold. @threshold_tokens@ compares
against the freshest step's measured occupancy (`usage.input_tokens + output_tokens`); @prompt@
frames the summarization step; @keep_recent@ is how many trailing history elements survive
verbatim — extended forward to a clean `turn` boundary, so a call turn is never severed from the
results that answer it."
data compaction(threshold_tokens: integer, prompt: string, keep_recent: integer)

@"The default compaction policy both session shapes fall back to: compact past 100k tokens,
summarize under the standard self-note prompt, keep the last 8 history elements verbatim."
agent default_compaction() -> compaction {
  compaction(
    threshold_tokens = 100000,
    prompt = "Summarize the following conversation for your own future reference: keep every fact, decision, open question, and personal detail you might need again; drop the pleasantries and the dead ends. Answer with the summary only.",
    keep_recent = 8,
  )
}

@"The verdict on a measured context occupancy: still under the threshold, with the room left."
data within_budget(@"Tokens still free under the threshold." remaining: integer)

@"The verdict on a measured context occupancy: over the threshold — time to compact."
data over_budget(@"The measured occupancy that tripped the threshold." used: integer)

// The budget verdict. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type budget = within_budget | over_budget

@"Classify a measured context occupancy against the policy threshold — the compaction machinery's
one numeric comparison; everything downstream dispatches on the returned sum."
agent classify_budget(used: integer, policy: compaction) -> budget {
  if (used < policy.threshold_tokens) {
    within_budget(remaining = policy.threshold_tokens - used)
  } else {
    over_budget(used = used)
  }
}

@"The integer at a provider's usage field, or 0 when the field is absent — a provider that omits a
count still yields a usable measurement. Providers read their wire usage through this."
agent token_count(node: unknown) -> integer {
  match (node) {
    case integer(value) -> value
    case _ -> 0
  }
}

@"The first index at or after @start@ whose element is a plain `turn` — the clean cut for `compact`:
a `call_turn` must never be severed from the `result_turn` that answers it (a provider replays them
as a matched pair), so the kept tail always starts on a turn. No turn after @start@ means nothing
safely survives, and the whole history summarizes."
agent turn_boundary(history: array[types.message], start: integer) -> integer {
  for (let entry in array.slice(target = history, start = start, end = array.length(target = history)), var offset: integer = 0) {
    match (entry) {
      case types.turn(role => _, text => _, files => _) -> { break start + offset }
      case types.call_turn(calls => _) -> { next with { offset = offset + 1 } }
      case types.result_turn(results => _) -> { next with { offset = offset + 1 } }
    }
  } then (_entries) { array.length(target = history) }
}

@"A role as the word a SUMMARY reads — not a wire token: this is the label a summarizing model sees on
a transcript line, so it is chosen for a reader and no provider's vocabulary is involved."
agent role_label(role: types.role) -> string {
  match (role) {
    case types.user_role(_) -> "user"
    case types.model_role(_) -> "model"
  }
}

@"A history element as summarizer-input text: turns as role-prefixed lines, tool traffic as compact
notes — enough for the summary to preserve what happened, nothing wire-shaped."
agent render_message(entry: types.message) -> string {
  match (entry) {
    case types.turn(role => role, text => text, files => files) -> {
      let notes = for (let attachment in files) { next f" [file ${json.stringify(value = attachment)}]" }
      let joined_notes = string.join(parts = notes, separator = "")
      f"${role_label(role = role)}: ${text}${joined_notes}"
    }
    case types.call_turn(calls => calls) -> {
      let names = for (let call in calls) { next call.name }
      let joined_names = string.join(parts = names, separator = ", ")
      f"model: (called ${joined_names})"
    }
    case types.result_turn(results => results) -> {
      let texts = for (let result in results) { next f"${result.name} -> ${result.text}" }
      let joined_texts = string.join(parts = texts, separator = "\n")
      f"tools: ${joined_texts}"
    }
  }
}

@"Compress a conversation to fit its budget again: everything before the kept tail (the last
@policy.keep_recent@ elements, extended to a clean turn boundary) is summarized by one tool-less
model step under @policy.prompt@, and the summary replaces it as a single opening turn. A history
that is all tail comes back unchanged. Exported on its own so a hand-rolled loop can compact too —
`serve_session` is this plus the metering that decides when."
agent compact(history: array[types.message], policy: compaction) -> array[types.message] {
  let count = array.length(target = history)
  let wanted = if (count < policy.keep_recent) { 0 } else { count - policy.keep_recent }
  let cut = turn_boundary(history = history, start = wanted)
  if (cut == 0) {
    history
  } else {
    let head = array.slice(target = history, start = 0, end = cut)
    let tail = array.slice(target = history, start = cut, end = count)
    let rendered = for (let entry in head) { next render_message(entry = entry) }
    let joined = string.join(parts = rendered, separator = "\n")
    // The summarization step is a MODEL call, so it can fail like any other. A failed compaction must
    // neither lose the conversation nor crash a long-lived loop: catch the provider failure `reply`
    // re-raises and skip compaction this time (return the history unchanged); the next over-budget turn
    // tries again. This is the one place `compact` chooses to absorb `step_error` rather than propagate it.
    use handler {
      request prelude.throw(error: step_error) -> never { break history }
    }
    let summary = reply(history = [types.turn(
      role = types.user_role(),
      text = f"${policy.prompt}\n\n${joined}",
      files = [],
    )])
    array.concat(
      left = [types.turn(role = types.user_role(), text = f"[Summary of the earlier conversation]\n${summary}", files = [])],
      right = tail,
    )
  }
}

@"Raised once per incoming message of a served session; `serve_session`'s handler answers it with
the model's reply. The app's transport bridge performs it — fold any routing detail (a channel id, a
thread ts) into @content@ on the way in, route the returned reply on the way out."
request session_message(@"The incoming message, routing details folded in." content: string, @"The message's attachments." files: array[file]) -> string

@"A served session's durable state: the running conversation and the freshest measured context
occupancy — 0 until a step has run, and again right after a compaction until the next step
re-measures."
data session_state(conversation: array[types.message], used_tokens: integer)

@"One served turn's outcome: the reply, the advanced conversation, the freshest measurement, and every
tool call the turn made (`tool_events` — the loop feeds each failure back to the model, and this is
where the app hears about it too; empty when the turn called no tool)."
data session_turn(reply: string, conversation: array[types.message], used_tokens: integer, tool_events: array[tool_event] ?= [])

@"A turn the provider FAILED mid-way: the conversation as far as it got (completed call / result
exchanges included), the step error, and the tool calls that DID run before it (a failure at step
five does not un-run steps one to four). `take_turn` hands failure back as a VALUE so a long-lived
caller — an app-owned event loop — can ABSORB it as one match arm (annotate the conversation, notify
a channel, await the next event) instead of unwinding; a one-shot caller re-raises it
(`infer_with_tools`, `serve_session`)."
data failed_turn(conversation: array[types.message], error: step_error, tool_events: array[tool_event] ?= [])

type turn_result = session_turn | failed_turn

@"ONE tool-less step over @history@, folded to a `turn_result`: the shared closing move of the
budget exhaustion and the empty-final renudge — both must commit to an answer without tools, and
both hand a provider failure back as a value. @events@ are the turn's tool calls so far, carried
through the close so a caller sees the whole turn's tool traffic whichever way it ended."
agent closing_step(history: array[types.message], events: array[tool_event] ?= []) -> turn_result {
  match (infer_step(history = history, tool_metas = [])) {
    case inference_failed(error => error) -> failed_turn(conversation = history, error = error, tool_events = events)
    case inferred(result => result) -> {
      let answer = match (result.step) {
        case types.step_final(text => text) -> text
        case types.step_call(calls => _) -> "(unexpected tool call without tools)"
      }
      session_turn(
        reply = answer,
        conversation = array.append(target = history, value = types.turn(role = types.model_role(), text = answer, files = [])),
        used_tokens = result.usage.input_tokens + result.usage.output_tokens,
        tool_events = events,
      )
    }
  }
}

@"THE turn loop — advance @conversation@ by one model turn and hand back a VALUE either way: up to
@max_steps@ steps against the ambient provider, tool batches dispatched in parallel, the model's
reply appended as a model turn, and the LAST step's `input + output` carried out as the context
measurement (each step's input covers the whole context, so only the freshest figure is the
occupancy — steps do not add). Budget spent, one forced tool-less close commits to an answer; an
EMPTY final is renudged once for a plain-text answer (an empty here is a lost answer, not a signal —
a caller whose protocol gives the empty reply meaning still receives the second empty as-is). A
provider failure at any step returns `failed_turn` rather than unwinding, so an app-owned event loop
absorbs it as one match arm. This is the ai package's ONE loop: sessions (`serve_session`) and
resident agents (an app-owned `observation` handler over a `region` nursery) are compositions over
it. The knobs are policy the caller owns: @tool_budget_milliseconds@ deadlines every tool call
(null = no deadline), @unbounded_tool_names@ exempts the named human-latency tools from that
deadline, and @nudge_empty_reply@ = false accepts an empty final as-is — for a caller
whose protocol gives the empty reply meaning (a watcher that stays quiet), so a quiet turn does not
pay the renudge's extra step."
agent take_turn[effect E lacks time.race_settled](
  conversation: array[types.message],
  tools: array[agent never -> unknown with {...E, prelude.throw[unknown]}],
  max_steps: integer,
  @"The per-tool-call budget in milliseconds — every call races it via `time.with_deadline`, and a timeout
comes back to the model as an error result. `null` (the default) sets no deadline." tool_budget_milliseconds: number | null ?= null,
  @"The metadata names of tools EXEMPT from the deadline race — the human-latency tools: an
approval-gated adapter that blocks on an operator's click, an MCP call that may park on an OAuth
authorization. The doctrine: a deadline bounds MACHINE latency, and a pending ask or park is visible
in the admin surface while it waits, so such a call may be left unbounded rather than cancelled
mid-ask. Defaults to none — every tool stays under the budget." unbounded_tool_names: array[string] ?= [],
  @"Whether an empty final reply is renudged once for a plain-text answer (the interactive default).
Pass `false` when the empty reply is a meaningful signal in your protocol." nudge_empty_reply: boolean ?= true,
) -> turn_result {
  let tool_metas = ensure_unique_names(metas = for (let tool in tools) { next reflection.get_metadata(value = tool) })
  let outcome = for (let _step in array.range(start = 0, end = max_steps), var state: session_turn = session_turn(reply = "", conversation = conversation, used_tokens = 0, tool_events = [])) {
    match (infer_step(history = state.conversation, tool_metas = tool_metas)) {
      case inference_failed(error => error) -> { break failed_turn(conversation = state.conversation, error = error, tool_events = state.tool_events) }
      case inferred(result => result) -> {
        let measured = result.usage.input_tokens + result.usage.output_tokens
        match (result.step) {
          case types.step_final(text => reply_text) -> {
            break session_turn(
              reply = reply_text,
              conversation = array.append(target = state.conversation, value = types.turn(role = types.model_role(), text = reply_text, files = [])),
              used_tokens = measured,
              tool_events = state.tool_events,
            )
          }
          case types.step_call(calls => calls) -> {
            let dispatches = parallel for (let call in calls) {
              next dispatch_one[E](tools = tools, call = call, budget_milliseconds = tool_budget_milliseconds, unbounded_tool_names = unbounded_tool_names)
            }
            let results = for (let one in dispatches) { next one.result }
            let events = for (let one in dispatches) { next one.event }
            let with_calls = array.append(target = state.conversation, value = types.call_turn(calls = calls))
            next with {
              state = session_turn(
                reply = "",
                conversation = array.append(target = with_calls, value = types.result_turn(results = results)),
                used_tokens = measured,
                tool_events = array.concat(left = state.tool_events, right = events),
              )
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  } then (_steps) {
    // Budget exhausted: the shared forced tool-less close.
    closing_step(history = array.append(target = state.conversation, value = budget_exhausted_turn()), events = state.tool_events)
  }
  // An EMPTY final is a lost answer IN AN INTERACTIVE EXCHANGE: renudge ONCE with an explicit ask,
  // and accept whatever the extra step says — a second empty comes back as-is (honestly) rather than
  // looping. A caller whose protocol gives the empty reply meaning (a quiet watcher) opts out with
  // @nudge_empty_reply@ = false and pays nothing. A failed turn passes through untouched.
  match (outcome) {
    case session_turn(reply => reply_text, conversation => advanced, used_tokens => used, tool_events => events) -> {
      if (reply_text == "" && nudge_empty_reply) {
        closing_step(history = array.append(target = advanced, value = empty_reply_nudge()), events = events)
      } else {
        session_turn(reply = reply_text, conversation = advanced, used_tokens = used, tool_events = events)
      }
    }
    case failed -> failed
  }
}

@"Serve a chat session for the extent of @continuation@ — the hand-rolled serve skeleton as one
provider, with token metering and automatic compaction. The handler's var carries the conversation
across messages (durable by construction: it survives a runtime restart like any run state); each
`session_message` runs one tool loop and answers with the reply. After each turn the freshest
measured occupancy is classified against @threshold_tokens@: over it, the conversation compacts
(`compact`) under @compact_prompt@, keeping the last @keep_recent@ elements verbatim. The ambient
provider (the `use <some provider>.provider(...)` line) and any @E@ handlers are installed by the caller, exactly as
for `infer_with_tools`; the continuation bridges its transport into `session_message` and typically
never returns (a `watch_messages` loop)."
agent serve_session[R, effect E lacks time.race_settled](
  @"The conversation the model starts from (PRIOR TURNS only — standing instructions belong in the
provider's `system` parameter, which every provider takes and compaction never touches; anything put
here is history, and the first compaction folds it into the summary)." context: array[types.message],
  @"The tools of every turn's loop." tools: array[agent never -> unknown with {...E, prelude.throw[unknown]}],
  @"The per-message step budget." max_steps: integer,
  @"The compaction policy — one `compaction(...)` datum; `null` (the default) is `default_compaction`." policy: compaction | null ?= null,
  @"The per-tool-call budget in milliseconds, or `null` (the default) for no deadline — see `take_turn`." tool_budget_milliseconds: number | null ?= null,
  @"Tool names exempt from the deadline race (human-latency tools) — see `take_turn`." unbounded_tool_names: array[string] ?= [],
  @"The session body: bridges a transport into `session_message`." continuation: agent (value: null) -> R with {...E, session_message},
) -> R {
  let effective_policy = match (policy) {
    case null -> default_compaction()
    case given -> given
  }
  use handler (var state: session_state = session_state(conversation = context, used_tokens = 0)) {
    request session_message(content: string, files: array[file]) {
      let asked = array.append(target = state.conversation, value = types.turn(role = types.user_role(), text = content, files = files))
      match (take_turn[E](conversation = asked, tools = tools, max_steps = max_steps, tool_budget_milliseconds = tool_budget_milliseconds, unbounded_tool_names = unbounded_tool_names)) {
        // A served session is one-shot per message, so a provider failure re-raises at the perform
        // site for the app's transport bridge (the `session_message` caller) to handle.
        case failed_turn(conversation => _, error => error, tool_events => _) -> { prelude.throw(error = error) }
        case session_turn(reply => reply_text, conversation => advanced, used_tokens => used, tool_events => _) -> {
          match (classify_budget(used = used, policy = effective_policy)) {
            case within_budget(remaining => _) -> {
              next reply_text with { state = session_state(conversation = advanced, used_tokens = used) }
            }
            case over_budget(used => _) -> {
              // The compacted context's occupancy is unknown until the next step measures it; 0 says
              // "not yet measured", never an estimate.
              next reply_text with { state = session_state(conversation = compact(history = advanced, policy = effective_policy), used_tokens = 0) }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  continuation(value = null)
}

// ── event vocabulary: observations and scheduled prompts ─────────────────────
//
// The resident-agent pattern: sources and watches fork into a `region` nursery and push
// `observation`s; a stateful handler injects each report and advances the conversation with
// `take_turn`. That serving handler is `serve_observations` below — the resident sibling of
// `serve_session`, and like it a composition OVER the package's one loop. The REGION wiring stays
// with the app (the nursery, its ceiling, the forks, and the `region.crashed` / `region.failed`
// interpretation):
// lifetime is the app's data, not this package's. Watches stay ordinary tools the app writes: a
// hand-authored adapter (with its own model-facing description) that approves and forks a fiber
// however the app sees fit.

@"THE event→model channel: a background fiber (an always-on source such as a chat watcher, or a
started watch) pushes @content@ (plus any @files@) from @source@ into the model's context.
FIRE-AND-FORGET (`-> null`): a fiber only reports — the app's serving handler injects the report as
a user turn, advances the conversation (`take_turn`), and routes the reply wherever it belongs.
@files@ ride the injection turn's `files` slot — the same text + files split as `types.turn` — so
the provider's existing inline policy shows the model any attached image. @author@ rides the
injection turn's `author`, so the triggered turn's requests carry the right abuse-attribution
metadata (`ai.attribution`)."
request observation(@"Which fiber is reporting (e.g. \"discord:<channel>\"); it labels the report's injection turn." source: string, @"The report to inject into the model's context." content: string, @"Any files attached to the report (a Discord / Slack attachment), injected on the report's turn so the provider inlines them for the model; defaults to none." files: array[file] ?= [], @"The opaque end-user tag of the human behind this report (e.g. a hashed chat-user id), or `null` for a system report (a scheduled tick)." author: string | null ?= null) -> null

@"A watch body reduced to its essence: on every occurrence of @schedule@, inject the freshly
computed @prompt@ into the model's context as an observation from @source@ — the scheduled
self-prompt every periodic watcher (check a repo, track a topic, look at a calendar) hand-rolled as
its own `time.watch` adapter. @prompt@ is an agent so each tick recomputes it (an env-configured
target re-reads, a date renders current); its effects @E@ flow to the app's handlers. Never returns:
fork it into the app's nursery and cancel its fiber to stop."
agent watch_prompt[effect E](
  @"Which watcher is reporting; labels the injection turn (e.g. \"repo-issues\")." source: string,
  @"Computes the report to inject, fresh at every occurrence." prompt: agent (value: null) -> string with E,
  @"When occurrences fire: `time.interval(...)` or `time.cron(...)`." schedule: time.schedule,
) -> never with {...E, observation} | io {
  agent tick(time: number) -> null with {...E, observation} {
    observation(source = source, content = prompt(value = null))
  }
  time.watch(schedule = schedule, deliver_to = tick)
}

// ── THE DESK: a keyed, resident conversation, as this package's DATA ──────────
//
// A resident program with ONE conversation is served by `serve_observations` below. A resident program
// with SEVERAL — a private assistant, a public-facing character, a table of workers spawned by name —
// falls off that API entirely, because it is built around one handler var holding one conversation.
// The first real app to need three of them descended to `take_turn` and hand-rolled about 460 lines of
// standing machinery: a bounded backlog per conversation, a dropped-count marker, an attachment shed, a
// note for the reply nobody delivered, a failure breaker — and the breaker THREE TIMES, once per
// conversation, because there was nowhere else to put it.
//
// A DESK is that machinery as data. It is: a conversation, its freshest context measurement, and the
// arrivals it is holding because the model could not be called. Nothing about a desk is keyed, ordered
// or routed — a program holds one desk per conversation however it likes (a handler var, a
// `record[desk]` keyed by worker name), and each arriving message is ONE call to `advance_desk`, whose
// answer is the new desk plus what happened. The app writes the routing, which is the part that is
// genuinely its own; everything a resident conversation learned the hard way is the default.
//
// FOUR BEHAVIOURS ARE BUILT IN, each of them paid for by an outage somewhere:
//
//   1. A BOUNDED BACKLOG. While the model cannot be called, arrivals are held — the newest
//      @backlog_retention@ of them — and the STORED CONVERSATION DOES NOT GROW. An outage of any
//      length therefore costs the conversation nothing, which is what stops the recovery turn from
//      being the one that fails on a context the outage itself piled up.
//   2. A DROPPED MARKER. What the bound discarded is SAID, in one derived line, when the backlog
//      rejoins the conversation — never appended, so it cannot accumulate. A model that knows twelve
//      messages are missing asks; one that does not answers confidently from the gap.
//   3. THE SHED. A call the provider REFUSED loses the attachments it carried, each loss noted in
//      place. A file's recorded content type is the sender's unverified claim, the provider checks the
//      bytes, and the mismatch is a refusal on the whole request — which then repeats on every later
//      call, because the refused message is re-sent whole. One mislabeled image can otherwise open an
//      outage no retry ever closes.
//   4. THE UNDELIVERED REPLY, SAID OUT LOUD. Under `announce_discarded_reply` a turn that ended with
//      text it did not deliver gains one line saying so, so a model that answered in its closing words
//      finds that out on its next turn instead of believing it spoke.
//
// The BREAKER is deliberately NOT here: it belongs to the provider seam, not to a conversation, so it
// is middleware (`with_breaker`) and one install covers every desk in the program. A desk simply sees
// its turn come back refused and files the arrival, which is the behaviour it would have wanted anyway.

@"The arrivals a desk is holding because the model was not answering: @messages@ is the bounded tail —
the newest @desk_policy.backlog_retention@ of them — and @dropped@ counts what fell off the front. It
lives beside the conversation rather than in it, which is what makes an outage cost the conversation
nothing at all."
data backlog(@"The newest held arrivals, oldest first." messages: array[types.message], @"How many older ones the bound discarded." dropped: integer)

@"An empty backlog — a desk holding nothing."
agent empty_backlog() -> backlog {
  backlog(messages = [], dropped = 0)
}

@"ONE desk: a resident conversation with its context measurement and whatever it is holding. Durable
by construction wherever the app keeps it (a handler var survives a runtime restart like any run
state). A program holds as many as it has conversations, keyed however it likes."
data desk(
  @"The conversation as it stands." conversation: array[types.message],
  @"The freshest measured context occupancy — 0 until a step has run, and again right after a compaction." used_tokens: integer,
  @"What arrived while the model could not be called." held: backlog,
)

@"A fresh desk: no conversation, nothing measured, nothing held. Standing instructions belong in the
provider's `system` parameter, which compaction never touches — so a desk starts genuinely empty."
agent new_desk() -> desk {
  desk(conversation = [], used_tokens = 0, held = empty_backlog())
}

@"ONE thing that arrived for a desk — the same text + files + author split as `types.turn`, plus
@source@, which labels the injected turn so the model can tell a chat message from a cron tick from
another agent's mail."
data arrival(
  @"Who is reporting (e.g. \"discord:<channel>\", \"cron:morning\"); labels the injected turn." source: string,
  @"The message body." content: string,
  @"Files riding the message." files: array[file] ?= [],
  @"The opaque end-user tag of the human behind it, or `null` for a system report." author: string | null ?= null,
)

@"What a turn's final text is for: DELIVERY. A non-blank reply goes to the desk's @deliver_to@ — the
ordinary chat shape, where the conversation owes one answer per message."
data deliver_reply()

@"What a turn's final text is for: NOTHING — the agent speaks through its posting tools instead, zero
times or five, to whichever channel a tool allows. The text is discarded, and the discard is ANNOUNCED
in the conversation (see the desk section's behaviour 4), because the failure this shape has is a model
believing it answered, not a model choosing silence."
data announce_discarded_reply()

// What becomes of a turn's final text. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type reply_policy = deliver_reply | announce_discarded_reply

@"How a desk behaves, as ONE datum — every knob a desk has, so a program states its policy once and
passes it to every desk (or a different one per desk; it is data)."
data desk_policy(
  @"The per-arrival step budget." max_steps: integer,
  @"The per-tool-call budget in milliseconds, or `null` for no deadline — see `take_turn`." tool_budget_milliseconds: number | null,
  @"Tool names exempt from the deadline race (human-latency tools) — see `take_turn`." unbounded_tool_names: array[string],
  @"When and how the conversation compacts." compaction_policy: compaction,
  @"How many arrivals the backlog holds before dropping the oldest." backlog_retention: integer,
  @"What a turn's final text is for." reply: reply_policy,
)

@"The default desk policy: 16 steps per arrival, a 60-second deadline on every tool call, no
exemptions, the default compaction, 24 held arrivals, and the reply delivered. The retention figure is
the bound on what an outage costs the returning conversation — a night of messages against a dead
provider comes back as the newest two dozen plus one line saying how many were dropped."
agent default_desk_policy() -> desk_policy {
  desk_policy(
    max_steps = 16,
    tool_budget_milliseconds = 60000.0,
    unbounded_tool_names = [],
    compaction_policy = default_compaction(),
    backlog_retention = 24,
    reply = deliver_reply(),
  )
}

@"What one arrival did to a desk: the model ANSWERED. @reply@ is the turn's final text — blank when the
model chose silence, and already delivered when the policy says to deliver."
data answered(@"The turn's final text; blank means the model had nothing to say." reply: string)

@"What one arrival did to a desk: the model was NOT CALLED AT ALL — a breaker is open — so the arrival
went into the backlog and no model call was spent. @held@ and @dropped@ are the backlog as it now
stands."
data filed(@"How many arrivals the desk is now holding." held: integer, @"How many the bound has discarded in total." dropped: integer)

@"What one arrival did to a desk: the model was called and the step FAILED. The arrival is in the
backlog (so nothing is lost), the stored conversation is untouched (so the outage costs it nothing),
and the attachments of a REFUSED call have been shed. The error rides out for the app to report on;
the desk has already survived it."
data step_failed(@"The step error the provider surfaced." error: step_error)

// What one arrival did to a desk. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type desk_outcome = answered | filed | step_failed

@"What `advance_desk` hands back: the desk as it now stands, what happened, and the turn's tool calls.
The state and the outcome move TOGETHER — a filed arrival advances the backlog and not the
conversation, an answered one advances the conversation and clears the backlog — so they are one value
and the app writes its var from `state` without re-deciding anything."
data desk_turn(
  @"The desk after this arrival — write it back." state: desk,
  @"What happened." outcome: desk_outcome,
  @"Every tool call the turn made, and how each ended." tool_events: array[tool_event],
)

@"The line that stands where one message's attachments were, in the message itself — the loss is
visible exactly where the file used to be, and it says what to do about it, because a model that knows
a file was shed can ask for a re-post, while one that does not will confidently describe an image it
cannot see."
agent shed_note(@"How many attachments this message loses." count: integer) -> string {
  f"[${string.to_string(value = count)} attachment(s) shed from this message: they rode a model call the provider refused, and a refused attachment would be refused again on every later call. If a file matters, ask for it to be posted again.]"
}

@"One tool result with its files shed and the loss noted in its text — the result-shaped half of
`shed_attachments`, kept beside it."
agent shed_result(@"The tool result to shed." result: types.tool_result) -> types.tool_result {
  match (array.length(target = result.files)) {
    case 0 -> result
    case count -> types.tool_result(name = result.name, ref = result.ref, text = f"${result.text}\n${shed_note(count = count)}", files = [])
  }
}

@"ONE message with its attachments shed and the loss noted in place; a message carrying none returns
untouched. All three message shapes are covered, because a user turn's files and a tool result's files
both inline into the request — either can be the file a provider refuses."
agent shed_attachments(@"The message to shed." message: types.message) -> types.message {
  match (message) {
    case types.turn(role => role, text => text, files => files, author => author) -> {
      match (array.length(target = files)) {
        case 0 -> message
        case count -> types.turn(role = role, text = f"${text}\n${shed_note(count = count)}", files = [], author = author)
      }
    }
    case types.call_turn(calls => _) -> message
    case types.result_turn(results => results) -> types.result_turn(results = for (let result in results) {
      next shed_result(result = result)
    })
  }
}

@"THE shed decision, made once from the outage classification: a REFUSED call's messages lose their
attachments, a passing failure's messages keep every one. `needs_operator` is exactly the family that
repeats deterministically — the same request meets the same refusal however long anyone waits — and an
attachment is the one part of the request whose acceptability an app cannot test before sending. A
`waits_out` failure sheds nothing: a rate limit is not the image's fault. The imprecision inside the
refused family is accepted with open eyes (a revoked key sheds files that were innocent), because
telling the-file-did-it from the-key-did-it would mean parsing provider error prose, and a shed
attachment is a noted, re-askable loss while a poisoned conversation is a desk nothing revives."
agent shed_refused(@"What the failure said about who can end it." kind: outage_kind, @"The messages that rode the failed call." messages: array[types.message]) -> array[types.message] {
  match (kind) {
    case needs_operator(remedy => _) -> for (let message in messages) {
      next shed_attachments(message = message)
    }
    case waits_out(_) -> messages
  }
}

@"File one arrival into a desk's backlog, bounded: keep the newest @retention@ and count what fell off
the front. Written as arithmetic on the window rather than as a branch, so there is no is-it-full-yet
case to get wrong — an unfilled backlog slices from 0 and drops nothing."
agent hold_arrival(@"The backlog as it stands." held: backlog, @"The message to file." message: types.message, @"How many to keep." retention: integer) -> backlog {
  let grown = array.append(target = held.messages, value = message)
  let overflow = math.max(left = 0, right = array.length(target = grown) - retention)
  backlog(
    messages = array.slice(target = grown, start = overflow, end = array.length(target = grown)),
    dropped = held.dropped + overflow,
  )
}

@"The ONE line that stands in for what a backlog's bound discarded, or no line at all when it discarded
nothing. It is DERIVED from the count on every attempt rather than appended to the stored conversation,
so an outage that spans ten failed probes still produces one line and not ten; the turn that finally
ANSWERS bakes that single line in and clears the count with the backlog it stood for."
agent dropped_marker(@"How many arrivals the bound discarded." dropped: integer) -> array[types.message] {
  match (dropped) {
    case 0 -> []
    case _ -> [types.turn(role = types.user_role(), text = f"[${string.to_string(value = dropped)} earlier message(s) also arrived while the model was unreachable and were dropped, oldest first, to keep this context workable. They are gone — if something seems to be missing, ask rather than guess.]", files = [])]
  }
}

@"The conversation ONE turn actually runs on: the desk's stored conversation, plus the derived
dropped-marker and whatever the backlog is holding. This is where a held backlog rejoins the
conversation, and it happens at the ATTEMPT, not at the filing, which is what keeps a desk that cannot
be served frozen."
agent pending_conversation(@"The desk." state: desk) -> array[types.message] {
  if (array.length(target = state.held.messages) == 0 && state.held.dropped == 0) {
    state.conversation
  } else {
    array.concat(
      left = array.concat(left = state.conversation, right = dropped_marker(dropped = state.held.dropped)),
      right = state.held.messages,
    )
  }
}

@"The prefix on a message that was FILED rather than answered, so a model coming back can see at a
glance which arrivals nobody replied to."
agent unanswered_note() -> string {
  "[filed while the model was unreachable — nobody answered this] "
}

@"One arrival as the turn it becomes: who it is from, then the body. @note@ prefixes it when the
arrival is filed rather than answered; it is the empty string on the ordinary path."
agent arrival_turn(@"The arrival." arrival: arrival, @"The filed-unanswered prefix, or empty." note: string) -> types.message {
  types.turn(
    role = types.user_role(),
    text = f"${note}Message from ${arrival.source}:\n${arrival.content}",
    files = arrival.files,
    author = arrival.author,
  )
}

@"The conversation a turn leaves behind, with ONE line appended when the turn ended with text that
nothing delivered. A BLANK final text gains nothing: ending silently is a legitimate move under this
policy, and annotating silence would be a nag. The line is the only condition tested, deliberately:
WHO the text was for is the model's judgement, and code that tried to infer it would be guessing."
agent mark_undelivered_reply(@"The conversation as the turn advanced it." conversation: array[types.message], @"The turn's final text." reply: string) -> array[types.message] {
  if (string.is_blank(value = reply)) {
    conversation
  } else {
    array.append(target = conversation, value = types.turn(role = types.user_role(), text = "[that turn's final text was not delivered: a turn's closing words reach nobody. If it was meant for someone, say it with the tool that reaches them.]", files = []))
  }
}

@"ADVANCE ONE DESK by one arrival — the whole of a resident conversation's per-message behaviour, as a
function from a desk and an arrival to the next desk. The arrival injects as a user turn onto the
desk's conversation PLUS whatever the backlog holds; one `take_turn` runs under `with_context(inject =
persona)`, so a per-turn persona re-derives at every model step and is never persisted; a blank final
is not renudged (silence is a legitimate answer for a resident). Then, by outcome:

— ANSWERED: the reply is delivered or discarded-and-announced per @policy.reply@, the backlog is
  cleared (it rejoined the conversation on this turn), and an over-budget conversation compacts.
— REFUSED BY A BREAKER (`with_breaker` is installed and open): no model call was spent — the arrival
  is filed into the bounded backlog and the stored conversation is untouched.
— FAILED: the same filing, plus the SHED — a call the provider refused loses the attachments it
  carried, across the whole backlog, because everything in it rode that call and re-rides every later
  one. The stored conversation stays frozen here too: a failed turn's partial tool work is discarded
  rather than preserved, because preserving it grows an outage's cost with the outage's length and
  re-sends whatever the provider refused. The arrival is not lost — it is in the backlog, and the turn
  that recovers reads it.

Tools and @persona@ are ordinary parameters, so a program with several desks passes a different set
and a different voice per desk without holding either in the desk's state."
agent advance_desk[effect E lacks time.race_settled](
  @"The desk as it stands." state: desk,
  @"What arrived." arrival: arrival,
  @"This desk's tool set — the whole of what this conversation can do." tools: array[agent never -> unknown with {...E, prelude.throw[unknown]}],
  @"Computes this desk's persona note, fresh at every model step (`with_context`). Return \"\" for none." persona: agent (value: null) -> string with {...E, infer_step},
  @"Where a delivered reply goes. Never called under `announce_discarded_reply`." deliver_to: agent (reply: string) -> null with E,
  @"The desk's policy; `null` (the default) is `default_desk_policy`." policy: desk_policy | null ?= null,
) -> desk_turn {
  let rules = match (policy) {
    case null -> default_desk_policy()
    case given -> given
  }
  let injected = array.append(target = pending_conversation(state = state), value = arrival_turn(arrival = arrival, note = ""))
  agent one_turn(value: null) -> turn_result with {...E, infer_step, prelude.throw[duplicate_tool]} | io {
    take_turn[E](
      conversation = injected,
      tools = tools,
      max_steps = rules.max_steps,
      tool_budget_milliseconds = rules.tool_budget_milliseconds,
      unbounded_tool_names = rules.unbounded_tool_names,
      nudge_empty_reply = false,
    )
  }
  match (with_context(inject = persona, continuation = one_turn)) {
    case failed_turn(conversation => _, error => error, tool_events => events) -> {
      // A refusal and a failure differ in exactly two places — whether anything was SENT (so whether
      // an attachment can have been refused) and what the app is told — and in nothing else, which is
      // why one arm serves both.
      let refusal = breaker_refusal(error = error)
      let kind = match (refusal) {
        case null -> classify_outage(error = error)
        case _ -> waits_out()
      }
      let grown = hold_arrival(held = state.held, message = arrival_turn(arrival = arrival, note = unanswered_note()), retention = rules.backlog_retention)
      let settled = backlog(messages = shed_refused(kind = kind, messages = grown.messages), dropped = grown.dropped)
      let outcome = match (refusal) {
        case null -> step_failed(error = error)
        case _ -> filed(held = array.length(target = settled.messages), dropped = settled.dropped)
      }
      desk_turn(
        state = desk(conversation = state.conversation, used_tokens = state.used_tokens, held = settled),
        outcome = outcome,
        tool_events = events,
      )
    }
    case session_turn(reply => final_text, conversation => advanced, used_tokens => used, tool_events => events) -> {
      let noted = match (rules.reply) {
        case deliver_reply(_) -> {
          if (string.is_blank(value = final_text)) { null } else { deliver_to(reply = final_text) }
          advanced
        }
        case announce_discarded_reply(_) -> mark_undelivered_reply(conversation = advanced, reply = final_text)
      }
      // Annotated BEFORE the budget is classified, so the note is part of what compaction summarizes
      // rather than something that outlives it.
      let carried = match (classify_budget(used = used, policy = rules.compaction_policy)) {
        case within_budget(remaining => _) -> desk(conversation = noted, used_tokens = used, held = empty_backlog())
        case over_budget(used => _) -> desk(conversation = compact(history = noted, policy = rules.compaction_policy), used_tokens = 0, held = empty_backlog())
      }
      desk_turn(state = carried, outcome = answered(reply = final_text), tool_events = events)
    }
  }
}

@"The empty persona: no per-step injection. `with_context` skips the extra turn for an empty note, so a
desk that wants no persona pays nothing for having the parameter."
agent no_persona(value: null) -> string {
  ""
}

@"Serve a SINGLE resident conversation's observations for the extent of @continuation@ — the simple
form: one desk behind one handler var, each `observation` advancing it (`advance_desk`) and the reply
leaving through @deliver_to@. THE RESIDENT PROTOCOL IS BUILT IN: an empty (blank) reply means 'nothing
to say' — it is not delivered and not renudged, so a quiet watcher tick costs exactly one model step.
Every desk behaviour comes with it (a bounded backlog, the dropped marker, the shed).

FOR MORE THAN ONE CONVERSATION, GO TO THE DESK API. This serves exactly one: a program that grows a
second addressee — a public-facing character beside a private one, a table of workers — cannot express
it here, because there is one var and one tool set. `advance_desk` is this same behaviour with the
routing left to you, and `with_breaker` is the outage policy for ALL of your desks at once.

A provider failure is ABSORBED (a resident must outlive a failed step) and NOTIFIED ONCE, on the
transition: the first failure after a run of good turns delivers one line, the failures after it
deliver nothing, and the turn that recovers delivers one line back. Per-turn notification is what
turned a delivery backlog plus a standing outage into sixteen identical messages in seconds; the
transition test is the fix, and installing `with_breaker` additionally stops the doomed calls."
agent serve_observations[R, effect E lacks time.race_settled](
  @"The tools of every turn's loop." tools: array[agent never -> unknown with {...E, prelude.throw[unknown]}],
  @"The per-observation step budget." max_steps: integer,
  @"Where a turn's non-empty reply (and the two outage lines) is delivered." deliver_to: agent (reply: string) -> null with E,
  @"The compaction policy — one `compaction(...)` datum; `null` (the default) is `default_compaction`." policy: compaction | null ?= null,
  @"The per-tool-call budget in milliseconds, or `null` (the default) for no deadline — see `take_turn`." tool_budget_milliseconds: number | null ?= null,
  @"Tool names exempt from the deadline race (human-latency tools) — see `take_turn`." unbounded_tool_names: array[string] ?= [],
  @"The resident body: forks its sources and typically never returns (`region.watch`)." continuation: agent (value: null) -> R with {...E, observation},
) -> R {
  let effective_policy = match (policy) {
    case null -> default_compaction()
    case given -> given
  }
  let rules = desk_policy(
    max_steps = max_steps,
    tool_budget_milliseconds = tool_budget_milliseconds,
    unbounded_tool_names = unbounded_tool_names,
    compaction_policy = effective_policy,
    backlog_retention = default_desk_policy().backlog_retention,
    reply = deliver_reply(),
  )
  // `announced` is the transition test, and the whole of the flood fix: a notice fires when it flips.
  use handler (var state: desk = new_desk(), var announced: boolean = false) {
    request observation(source: string, content: string, files: array[file], author: string | null) {
      let advanced = advance_desk[E](
        state = state,
        arrival = arrival(source = source, content = content, files = files, author = author),
        tools = tools,
        persona = no_persona,
        deliver_to = deliver_to,
        policy = rules,
      )
      match (advanced.outcome) {
        case step_failed(error => error) -> {
          if (announced) {
            null
          } else {
            deliver_to(reply = f"(the model is not answering — this turn was dropped and what arrives is kept: ${json.stringify(value = error)})")
          }
          next null with { state = advanced.state, announced = true }
        }
        case filed(held => _, dropped => _) -> {
          next null with { state = advanced.state, announced = announced }
        }
        case answered(reply => _) -> {
          if (announced) {
            deliver_to(reply = "(the model is answering again — picking up from where it stopped.)")
          } else {
            null
          }
          next null with { state = advanced.state, announced = false }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  continuation(value = null)
}

// ── THE DESK TABLE: keyed resident desks, and the generation that survives a reused name ──────────
//
// One desk is `advance_desk`, and a program with a FIXED handful of them — a private core, a
// public-facing herald — holds one var apiece and routes by hand, which is right: two conversations are
// two names, not a collection. A program that spawns conversations BY NAME AT RUNTIME — a table of
// workers admitted and dismissed while it runs — has a genuine collection, and every such program
// hand-wrote the same two things around `advance_desk`: a `record[desk]` keyed by the name, and a
// GENERATION check that throws away an arrival left over from a name that has since been reused.
//
// WHY THE GENERATION EARNS A PLACE. A worker is a name, and a name is reusable: dismiss `scribe`, admit
// `scribe` again for a different errand, and the second scribe must inherit neither the first one's
// conversation nor the first one's still-in-flight mail. Nothing about the NAME tells the two apart, so
// the name alone cannot — and a monotonic generation stamped at each admit does. The roster mints a
// fresh higher number every time a name is admitted; each arrival carries the generation it was
// dispatched under; the table remembers the generation the conversation it holds belongs to. An arrival
// OLDER than what the table holds is the previous incarnation's straggler — dropped, `stale`, changing
// nothing — an arrival NEWER is a reused name whose held conversation is superseded, so that desk is
// discarded and a fresh one takes the turn, and the EQUAL case is the ordinary one: same incarnation,
// one more message. Without this a relaunched `scribe` answers the dead scribe's last mail out of the
// dead scribe's context, which is precisely the mix-up a reusable name invites.
//
// WHO OWNS THE GENERATION: the app's roster, not the table — and that split is the load-bearing choice,
// the same one `with_breaker` made. The number is minted where a name is ADMITTED, a site that also
// validates the name, refuses the reserved ones, holds the brief and lists the roster — concerns the
// table has no business absorbing. That site sits ABOVE every desk (its mutators are tools, whose
// perform escalates from the desk that ran the turn); the table sits at the desk it serves, below, and
// a var below cannot mint for a handler above. So the generation is PASSED IN to `advance_in_table`,
// read from the roster at dispatch, and the table only stores it and compares. The table mints nothing
// and offers no `bump` — a second counter beside the roster's would be a second source of truth for one
// fact, and the wrong one to read.
//
// NOT FOR A PROGRAM'S FIXED DESKS. A core and a herald are two vars, not a one-and-two keyed table, and
// folding them in would buy nothing and cost a generation nobody reuses. The table earns its keep
// exactly where keys come and go.
//
// A KEYED CONTAINER PLUS ONE RULE — NOT A REIMPLEMENTATION. The per-arrival move is `advance_desk`'s,
// whole and unchanged: the bounded backlog, the shed, the compaction, the breaker's refusal, one call
// per served arrival. The table adds the lookup, the fresh-desk default, and the staleness verdict, and
// delegates every byte of the progression back to `advance_desk`. It is the routing a resident
// multi-agent program kept re-deriving, and nothing more.

@"One key's stored desk, tagged with the launch @generation@ its conversation belongs to — the table's
cell. The table discards a cell whose generation an arrival supersedes (the name was reused), so a
reused key never inherits the previous incarnation's conversation. Internal to the table's machinery; an
app reaches a desk through `advance_in_table`, never this."
data keyed_desk(@"The generation this cell's conversation belongs to." generation: integer, @"The desk as it stands." desk: desk)

@"A TABLE of keyed resident desks: for each key, the desk and the generation it belongs to, in one
value. OPAQUE by contract — a program builds it with `new_desk_table`, advances a key through
`advance_in_table`, drops one with `forget_desk`, reads a key's generation with `desk_generation`, and
reaches into @cells@ for nothing else. Durable by construction wherever the app keeps it (a handler var
survives a runtime restart like any run state); a program holds one per collection of dynamically keyed
conversations."
data desk_table(@"The keyed cells; OPAQUE — use the table's own functions, not this field." cells: record[keyed_desk])

@"A fresh, empty desk table — no keys, no desks. A program installs one per collection of dynamically
keyed conversations (a worker table); its fixed desks stay their own vars."
agent new_desk_table() -> desk_table {
  desk_table(cells = record.empty())
}

@"What `advance_in_table` did: the arrival was SERVED — a desk took the turn (the key's own, or a fresh
one when the key was new or its generation superseded). @turn@ is the whole `advance_desk` result — the
desk's per-arrival outcome (`answered` / `filed` / `step_failed`), its new state (already written back
into the returned table), and its tool events — so a program that cares about the desk-level outcome
reads it here, and one that does not just writes the table back."
data advanced(@"The desk's turn — `advance_desk`'s full result." turn: desk_turn)

@"What `advance_in_table` did: NOTHING — the arrival's generation was OLDER than the one the table holds
for its key, so it is a straggler from a superseded incarnation of a reused name. It is dropped, the
table is left exactly as it was, and no model call is spent. @arrival_generation@ is what the arrival
carried and @current_generation@ is what the table holds, so a program can log precisely which
incarnation's message it discarded."
data stale(@"The generation the dropped arrival carried." arrival_generation: integer, @"The generation the table currently holds for the key." current_generation: integer)

// What one arrival did to a desk table. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type table_outcome = advanced | stale

@"What `advance_in_table` hands back: the table as it now stands, what happened, and the turn's tool
calls. The table and the outcome move TOGETHER, exactly as `desk_turn` pairs a desk with its outcome — a
served arrival advanced some key's desk and @table@ has it written back, a stale one changed nothing and
@table@ is the one that went in — so the app writes its var from @table@ without re-deciding anything,
and reads @tool_events@ (empty on `stale`, nothing having run) the same way whichever way the turn
went."
data table_turn(@"The table after this arrival — write it back." table: desk_table, @"What happened." outcome: table_outcome, @"Every tool call the turn made, and how each ended; empty on `stale`." tool_events: array[tool_event])

@"ADVANCE ONE KEY'S DESK in a table by one arrival — the keyed sibling of `advance_desk`, with the
reused-name generation rule folded in. Look @key@ up in @table@, then decide by how @generation@ (the
arrival's, minted by the app's roster at admit and carried here) compares with what the table holds:

— KEY ABSENT: a fresh `new_desk` takes the turn, stored under @generation@.
— GENERATION OLDER than the cell's: the arrival is a straggler from a superseded incarnation of a reused
  name — DROPPED (`stale`), the table unchanged, no model call spent.
— GENERATION EQUAL: the ordinary case — the held desk takes the turn, one more message in the same
  incarnation's conversation.
— GENERATION NEWER than the cell's: the name was reused and the held desk is the PREVIOUS incarnation's,
  so it is discarded and a fresh `new_desk` takes the turn under @generation@ — a reused name never
  inherits the dismissed one's conversation or mandate.

The turn itself is `advance_desk`'s, whole and unchanged: @tools@, @persona@, @deliver_to@ and @policy@
are its parameters, passed straight through, so a table of workers gives each key its own voice and tool
set per dispatch without holding either in the table's state. This function adds only the lookup, the
fresh-desk default and the staleness verdict; the backlog, the shed and the compaction are the desk's."
agent advance_in_table[effect E lacks time.race_settled](
  @"The table as it stands." table: desk_table,
  @"The key whose desk this arrival is for (a worker name — already normalized by the app)." key: string,
  @"The arrival's launch generation, minted by the app's roster at admit and read at dispatch." generation: integer,
  @"What arrived." arrival: arrival,
  @"This key's tool set for this turn — the whole of what this conversation can do." tools: array[agent never -> unknown with {...E, prelude.throw[unknown]}],
  @"Computes this key's persona note, fresh at every model step (`with_context`). Return \"\" for none." persona: agent (value: null) -> string with {...E, infer_step},
  @"Where a delivered reply goes. Never called under `announce_discarded_reply`." deliver_to: agent (reply: string) -> null with E,
  @"The desk's policy; `null` (the default) is `default_desk_policy`." policy: desk_policy | null ?= null,
) -> table_turn {
  match (record.get(target = table.cells, key = key)) {
    case null -> serve_in_table[E](table = table, key = key, generation = generation, base = new_desk(), arrival = arrival, tools = tools, persona = persona, deliver_to = deliver_to, policy = policy)
    case keyed_desk(generation => held_generation, desk => held) -> {
      if (generation < held_generation) {
        // The generation INVERSION: a straggler from an incarnation the table has already moved past.
        // The table is returned untouched — the whole point of the rule is that this arrival leaves no
        // trace on the conversation the current incarnation is building.
        table_turn(table = table, outcome = stale(arrival_generation = generation, current_generation = held_generation), tool_events = [])
      } else if (generation == held_generation) {
        // Same incarnation: the held desk takes the turn.
        serve_in_table[E](table = table, key = key, generation = generation, base = held, arrival = arrival, tools = tools, persona = persona, deliver_to = deliver_to, policy = policy)
      } else {
        // The name was reused (a higher generation): the held desk is the previous incarnation's, so it
        // is discarded and a fresh one takes the turn — the same "never inherit a superseded
        // conversation" a hand-rolled worker table wrote as `held.generation == generation`.
        serve_in_table[E](table = table, key = key, generation = generation, base = new_desk(), arrival = arrival, tools = tools, persona = persona, deliver_to = deliver_to, policy = policy)
      }
    }
  }
}

@"Run one arrival against @base@ (the desk the generation rule chose) through `advance_desk`, then write
the result back into @table@ under @key@ STAMPED with @generation@ — the served half of
`advance_in_table`, shared by its three spending arms (key absent, generation equal, generation newer).
Stamping the generation on write-back is what lets a fresh desk started for a reused name record the
incarnation it belongs to, so the next arrival compares against the right number. Internal to
`advance_in_table`."
agent serve_in_table[effect E lacks time.race_settled](
  @"The table to write the advanced desk back into." table: desk_table,
  @"The key to store under." key: string,
  @"The generation to stamp the stored cell with." generation: integer,
  @"The desk the arrival runs against (the key's own, or a fresh one)." base: desk,
  @"What arrived." arrival: arrival,
  @"This key's tool set for this turn." tools: array[agent never -> unknown with {...E, prelude.throw[unknown]}],
  @"Computes this key's persona note, fresh at every model step." persona: agent (value: null) -> string with {...E, infer_step},
  @"Where a delivered reply goes." deliver_to: agent (reply: string) -> null with E,
  @"The desk's policy; `null` is `default_desk_policy`." policy: desk_policy | null ?= null,
) -> table_turn {
  let turn = advance_desk[E](state = base, arrival = arrival, tools = tools, persona = persona, deliver_to = deliver_to, policy = policy)
  table_turn(
    table = desk_table(cells = record.set(target = table.cells, key = key, value = keyed_desk(generation = generation, desk = turn.state))),
    outcome = advanced(turn = turn),
    tool_events = turn.tool_events,
  )
}

@"Drop @key@ from the table — the RETIRE move. After it the table holds no desk and no generation for
the key, so the next arrival for it (should the name be admitted again) starts a fresh desk. A program
that retires a worker by dropping it from its roster forgets its desk here in the same breath. Forgetting
a key the table never held is a no-op."
agent forget_desk(@"The table." table: desk_table, @"The key to drop." key: string) -> desk_table {
  desk_table(cells = record.remove(target = table.cells, key = key))
}

@"The generation the table holds for @key@ — the incarnation its stored conversation belongs to — or
`null` when the table holds no desk for the key. A reader for observation and tests: the generation an
arrival is COMPARED against is the app's to mint and lives in its roster, and this reports what the table
last STORED, not what the roster now says. (The table offers no way to SET a generation for this reason —
the roster is the one source of truth for which incarnation a name is on.)"
agent desk_generation(@"The table." table: desk_table, @"The key to read." key: string) -> integer | null {
  match (record.get(target = table.cells, key = key)) {
    case null -> null
    case keyed_desk(generation => generation, desk => _) -> generation
  }
}

// ── the supervisor: a restart loop with backoff and same-reason suppression ────
//
// A resident that must OUTLIVE its own crashes needs a supervisor: run one session, and when it ends
// with a recoverable reason, wait a backoff and start a fresh one. The mechanism is generic — the
// session body, the restart-notice sink, and the backoff numbers are all DATA — so an app keeps every
// specific it owns: WHICH crashes are fatal (its session re-raises those PAST `supervise`, which only
// ever sees a RETURNED recoverable reason), WHAT a restart notice says, and WHERE it goes. This is the
// lifetime sibling of `serve_observations`: that one keeps a resident alive across a failed STEP; this
// one keeps it alive across a failed SESSION.

@"WHY one supervised attempt ended, split into the part that is COMPARED and the part that is READ.
@kind@ is the reason's stable identity — a short tag the app picks for the failure's shape (\"panic\",
\"discord-source-died\", a thrown error's constructor name) — and it is the only thing the backoff
looks at. @detail@ is the line a human reads and may carry anything: an id, a timestamp, a quoted
message. Splitting them is the point: comparing whole prose, which is what this replaced, means a
message carrying an id or a timestamp NEVER equals the previous one, so a sustained outage looks like
a fresh failure every time, the backoff resets to its floor on every restart, and the notice that was
meant to go quiet shouts identically forever. The set of an app's crash kinds is the app's own, which
is why @kind@ is a string here and not a sum this package could not have written."
data crash_reason(@"The reason's stable identity — the only thing compared." kind: string, @"The human line — free to vary." detail: string)

@"A restart whose reason is NEW: the previous attempt ended for a different reason, or this is the
first restart. The backoff is back at its floor and the notice is worth stating in full."
data first_failure(@"Why this attempt ended." reason: crash_reason)

@"A restart REPEATING the previous reason: the same @kind@ again, @streak@ times in a row now (2 on the
first repeat). The backoff is growing, and the sink can shorten this to one quiet line — or drop it —
because nobody needs the same sentence every thirty seconds."
data repeated_failure(@"Why this attempt ended." reason: crash_reason, @"How many attempts in a row have ended this way, counting this one." streak: integer)

// One restart, as the sink hears about it. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type restart_event = first_failure | repeated_failure

@"Supervise a restartable @session@ FOREVER: run one attempt, and when it ends by RETURNING a
recoverable `crash_reason`, call @on_restart@, wait the current backoff, and start a fresh attempt. The
backoff starts at @initial_delay_ms@ and grows by @factor@ each time the reason's KIND repeats (a
sustained outage), capped at @max_delay_ms@; a changed kind resets it to the floor. @on_restart@ hears
a `restart_event`, so a repeat is a distinguishable value carrying its streak rather than a boolean
flag beside a string. Fatal-vs-restartable is the SESSION's decision, not this loop's: a session that
hits an unrecoverable error re-raises it, and it propagates through `supervise` UNTOUCHED to a handler
the app installed above; `supervise` only ever sees the reason a session hands back by RETURNING, and
every such return is a restart. Returns `never` — the loop ends only when a session's fatal re-raise
unwinds past it."
agent supervise[effect E](
  @"One attempt: run the resident until it ends recoverably and RETURN the crash reason to restart from. A fatal error re-raises past `supervise` instead of returning." session: agent (value: null) -> crash_reason with E,
  @"The restart-notice sink, called once per restart. A `repeated_failure` carries its streak, so a repeat can be shortened or dropped." on_restart: agent (event: restart_event) -> null with E,
  @"The backoff floor in milliseconds — the first wait, and the wait a changed kind resets to. Default 30s." initial_delay_ms: number ?= 30000.0,
  @"The factor the backoff grows by while the same kind repeats. Default 2." factor: number ?= 2.0,
  @"The backoff ceiling in milliseconds — the growing delay never exceeds it. Default 15 minutes." max_delay_ms: number ?= 900000.0,
) -> never with E | io {
  // `streak` doubles as the have-we-run-before marker: 0 means no previous attempt, so the very first
  // crash is a `first_failure` whatever its kind is (including the empty one).
  forever (var delay: number = initial_delay_ms, var last_kind: string = "", var streak: integer = 0) {
    let reason = session(value = null)
    let repeated = streak > 0 && reason.kind == last_kind
    let run = if (repeated) { streak + 1 } else { 1 }
    let event = if (repeated) { repeated_failure(reason = reason, streak = run) } else { first_failure(reason = reason) }
    on_restart(event = event)
    time.sleep(milliseconds = delay)
    // Grow the delay while the kind repeats (a sustained outage); reset to the floor when it changes.
    let next_delay = if (repeated) { math.min(left = delay * factor, right = max_delay_ms) } else { initial_delay_ms }
    next with { delay = next_delay, last_kind = reason.kind, streak = run }
  }
}

// ── ambient-context middleware ────────────────────────────────────────────────

@"Middleware over the seam: EVERY `infer_step`, prepend @inject@'s freshly computed text as a user
turn and re-perform outward to the ambient provider — the generalization of the bot-side pattern
that re-injects the current time and a memory index each turn. Install it INSIDE the provider (the
provider's `use` first, then `use ai.with_context(inject = ...)`), so the re-performed step
reaches the model. An EMPTY injection skips the extra turn; the injected turn is never part of the
conversation a loop persists (it is derived fresh each step, so persisting it would double it).
`infer_object` passes through un-injected — structured extraction states its own context."
agent with_context[R, effect E](
  @"Computes the note to prepend this step — ambient state (the time, a memory index), fresh each step." inject: agent (value: null) -> string with {...E, infer_step},
  @"The program to run with the injection installed." continuation: agent (value: null) -> R with {...E, infer_step},
) -> R with E | infer_step {
  use handler {
    request infer_step(history: array[types.message], tool_metas: array[reflection.agent_metadata]) -> step_outcome {
      let note = inject(value = null)
      let injected = if (note == "") {
        history
      } else {
        array.concat(left = [types.turn(role = types.user_role(), text = note, files = [])], right = history)
      }
      // Re-performing escalates PAST this handler to the ambient provider — the outward re-perform
      // semantics a rethrown request uses.
      next infer_step(history = injected, tool_metas = tool_metas)
    }
  }
  continuation(value = null)
}

// ── the model BREAKER: middleware over the seam ───────────────────────────────
//
// ABSORBING a failed step — annotate, wait for the next event — is exactly right when the provider is
// having a bad minute, and exactly wrong when it is out for the evening. The two conditions that expose
// the difference arrive together: a delivery backlog releases a dozen queued messages at once while
// every model call is failing with a status that is NOT transient (a spent credit balance answers 400,
// so a provider's retry ladder spends no backoff at all). Each queued message then runs a full-speed
// failing turn and each one posts its outage line — sixteen identical messages into a channel in
// seconds. The backlog is the multiplier; the standing failure is the cause; the per-turn notice is the
// megaphone.
//
// IT IS MIDDLEWARE, NOT DESK STATE, and that is the load-bearing choice. Whether the provider is
// answering is a fact about the PROVIDER, so it belongs at the provider seam — one `use
// ai.with_breaker(...)` inside the provider install, and every desk, every session and every
// hand-rolled `take_turn` in the program passes through the one breaker by dynamic scope. Put it in a
// desk instead and a program with three desks holds three breakers, each discovering the same outage on
// its own, each announcing it on its own schedule, and each spending its own probes on a provider the
// other two already know is down.
//
// TWO LAYERS, DELIBERATELY NOT MIXED. A provider's own retry ladder is untouched underneath, because it
// answers a different question: it is how hard ONE call tries before giving up. The breaker answers
// whether to START a call at all. Keeping them separate is what lets the probe be honest about its
// price: a probe is a real step, so on a rate limit it can sit inside the provider's retry ladder for a
// minute before failing — which is exactly why the probes space themselves out even though the first
// one does not wait at all.

@"What a failed step says about who can end the outage: a PERSON must act. A 400 (a spent credit
balance, a model name the provider no longer serves), a 401 or a 403 (a wrong, revoked or
under-privileged key), a 404 (an unknown model) do not heal with time, so @remedy@ carries the one
clause that turns an evening of silence into a five-minute fix."
data needs_operator(@"What to check, in one clause." remedy: string)

@"What a failed step says about who can end the outage: NOBODY — it is the kind that passes. A rate
limit, a 5xx, a dropped connection, a reply the provider could not make sense of. The provider has
already spent its retry budget on it, so the only jobs left are to stop hammering while it lasts and to
say nothing an operator cannot act on."
data waits_out()

// What a failed step says about who can end the outage. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type outage_kind = needs_operator | waits_out

@"Classify a failed step by WHO CAN END IT. The statuses a person must act on are matched as LITERALS,
so the rule reads as the list it is; everything else waits. A `json.parse_error` and a `provider_error`
land in `waits_out` deliberately: they are not transient either, but there is no lever to hand an
operator for them, and sending someone to check a balance that is fine is worse than saying nothing
specific."
agent classify_outage(@"The failed step's error." error: step_error) -> outage_kind {
  match (error) {
    case http.status_error(status => status, body => _) -> {
      match (status) {
        case 400 -> needs_operator(remedy = "the provider is rejecting the request outright — check the API credit balance first, then whether the configured model name still exists")
        case 401 -> needs_operator(remedy = "the provider refused the API key — it is wrong, revoked or expired")
        case 403 -> needs_operator(remedy = "the provider refused the API key's permissions — check the key's plan and model access")
        case 404 -> needs_operator(remedy = "the provider does not know the configured model — check the model name")
        case _ -> waits_out()
      }
    }
    case http.fetch_error(message => _) -> waits_out()
    case json.parse_error(message => _) -> waits_out()
    case provider_error(source => _, message => _, transient => _, detail => _) -> waits_out()
  }
}

@"The two lines a breaker transition is worth saying, as ONE agent over the classification: what a
channel hears when the model stops answering. Both are fixed and model-free, so neither leaks anything
of a conversation; only the `needs_operator` one names plumbing, which is why an app posting to a
channel STRANGERS read should hand this `waits_out()` whatever the kind actually was and route the
remedy to the operator instead. A remedy is information for the person who can act on it and for
nobody else."
agent outage_line(@"What the failure said about who can end it." kind: outage_kind) -> string {
  match (kind) {
    case needs_operator(remedy => remedy) -> f"(the model is not answering, and it will not recover on its own: ${remedy}. I have stopped calling it — what arrives is kept, and I pick it up once it works again.)"
    case waits_out(_) -> "(the model is not answering. I have stopped calling it for now — what arrives is kept, and I keep trying periodically.)"
  }
}

@"The ONE line a channel hears when the breaker CLOSES: a probe got an answer. One line on the way
back, matching the one line on the way out — a returning provider must not read as a resumed flood."
agent recovery_line() -> string {
  "(the model is answering again — picking up from where it stopped.)"
}

@"The breaker OPENED: the model has stopped being called at all. @kind@ chose the wording and names the
remedy when there is one."
data breaker_opened(@"What the failure that opened it said about who can end it." kind: outage_kind)

@"The breaker CLOSED: a probe got an answer and calls have resumed. @kind@ is what the outage was LAST
classified as, so a sink can tell whoever it sent to check something that there is nothing left to
check."
data breaker_closed(@"What the outage was last classified as." kind: outage_kind)

// A breaker transition. Exactly two of these happen per outage, whatever it costs. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type breaker_event = breaker_opened | breaker_closed

@"How the breaker behaves. @failure_threshold@ consecutive failed steps open it; while open, one step
per probe interval is spent testing for recovery, starting at @probe_backoff_floor_milliseconds@ after
the first failed probe and doubling up to @probe_backoff_cap_milliseconds@."
data breaker_policy(
  @"How many CONSECUTIVE failed steps open the breaker." failure_threshold: integer,
  @"The wait before the SECOND probe of an outage — the ladder's floor, not the wait before the first, which is none." probe_backoff_floor_milliseconds: number,
  @"The longest an open breaker ever waits between probes." probe_backoff_cap_milliseconds: number,
)

@"The default breaker policy: three consecutive failures open it, the probe ladder runs from 30 seconds
to ten minutes. THREE, because one failure is noise and two can still be one bad minute, while three in
a row means the provider itself is out. TEN MINUTES, because a dead provider is then re-tested six
times an hour for the price of one step each, which is the standing cost of being wrong about an
outage."
agent default_breaker_policy() -> breaker_policy {
  breaker_policy(
    failure_threshold = 3,
    probe_backoff_floor_milliseconds = 30000.0,
    probe_backoff_cap_milliseconds = 600000.0,
  )
}

@"The breaker, CLOSED: the model is answering, and @consecutive_failures@ steps have failed back to
back since the last success (0 in the ordinary case)."
data model_answering(@"Failures since the last success; reset to 0 by any successful step." consecutive_failures: integer)

@"The breaker, OPEN: the provider is not being called. @kind@ is what the most recent failure said
about who can fix it (re-read at every failed probe, because an outage can change hands while the
breaker is open — a rate limit ends, a credit balance runs out). @next_probe@ is the earliest instant a
probe may run, and @next_delay@ is how far the one AFTER it gets pushed out: the ladder lives in the
state, not in a constant."
data model_down(
  @"What the last failure said about who can end this." kind: outage_kind,
  @"The earliest epoch-millisecond instant a probe may run." next_probe: number,
  @"How long the NEXT failed probe pushes the one after it out, in milliseconds." next_delay: number,
)

// The breaker's state. One per `with_breaker` install, not one per conversation. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type breaker_state = model_answering | model_down

@"The breaker's decision for one step: CALL the provider — either the breaker is closed (the ordinary
case) or it is open and the probe is due, so this step is spent on the one probe that tests for
recovery."
data call_now()

@"The breaker's decision for one step: do NOT call — the breaker is open and the probe is not due. The
open breaker rides the verdict so the caller never re-matches a state the decision already looked at."
data hold_off(@"The open breaker this step is refused by." down: model_down)

// What becomes of one step, decided once from the breaker state. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type breaker_verdict = call_now | hold_off

@"THE probe-due comparison, made ONCE and turned into a sum: an open breaker spends a step on a probe
when @now@ has reached its @next_probe@ and refuses every other; a closed breaker always calls. The
FIRST probe of an outage does not wait at all — the breaker opens with @next_probe@ already past, so a
three-failure blip costs the program the next step and no more."
agent may_call(@"The breaker as it stands." breaker: breaker_state, @"The current instant, in epoch milliseconds." now: number) -> breaker_verdict {
  match (breaker) {
    case model_answering(consecutive_failures => _) -> call_now()
    case down: model_down -> {
      if (now >= down.next_probe) {
        call_now()
      } else {
        hold_off(down = down)
      }
    }
  }
}

@"The breaker after ONE failed step: under the threshold it stays closed with the count raised; at the
threshold it OPENS, READY — @next_probe@ is @now@, so the next step to arrive probes immediately. An
already-open breaker climbs its ladder instead: the next probe is pushed out by the current delay, the
delay doubles up to the cap, and the kind is re-read from THIS failure."
agent after_failure(
  @"The breaker as it stands." breaker: breaker_state,
  @"The policy." policy: breaker_policy,
  @"What this failure said about who can end it." kind: outage_kind,
  @"The instant of the failed step, in epoch milliseconds." now: number,
) -> breaker_state {
  match (breaker) {
    case model_answering(consecutive_failures => before) -> {
      let failures = before + 1
      if (failures < policy.failure_threshold) {
        model_answering(consecutive_failures = failures)
      } else {
        model_down(kind = kind, next_probe = now, next_delay = policy.probe_backoff_floor_milliseconds)
      }
    }
    case down: model_down -> {
      model_down(
        kind = kind,
        next_probe = now + down.next_delay,
        next_delay = math.min(left = down.next_delay * 2.0, right = policy.probe_backoff_cap_milliseconds),
      )
    }
  }
}

@"The step error an OPEN breaker answers with — a refusal, not a failure: nothing was sent, so nothing
was refused by anyone but this program. @kind@ is what the outage was last classified as and
@retry_after_milliseconds@ is how long until the next probe is due, so a caller can say something true
without asking the breaker anything."
data breaker_refused(@"What the outage was last classified as." kind: outage_kind, @"How long until a probe is due, in milliseconds." retry_after_milliseconds: number)

@"The `step_error` an open breaker resolves the seam with: a `provider_error` whose producer is this
middleware, marked TRANSIENT (an open breaker is by definition a state that ends) and carrying the
typed `breaker_refused` as its detail. It rides the ordinary failure channel on purpose — a loop that
knows nothing of breakers still handles it as the step failure it is, and one that does reads the
detail out with `breaker_refusal`."
agent breaker_refusal_error(@"The open breaker." down: model_down, @"The current instant, in epoch milliseconds." now: number) -> step_error {
  provider_error(
    source = "ai.with_breaker",
    message = "the model was not called: this program's breaker is open after consecutive failed steps",
    transient = true,
    detail = breaker_refused(
      kind = down.kind,
      retry_after_milliseconds = math.max(left = 0.0, right = down.next_probe - now),
    ),
  )
}

@"Read a step error as the breaker's REFUSAL, or `null` when it is a genuine failure. The difference
matters wherever a caller would otherwise treat the two alike: a refusal spent no model call, sent
nothing and therefore refused no attachment, so a desk files the arrival and sheds nothing (see
`advance_desk`)."
agent breaker_refusal(@"The step error to read." error: step_error) -> breaker_refused | null {
  match (error) {
    case provider_error(source => _, message => _, transient => _, detail => detail) -> {
      match (detail) {
        case breaker_refused(kind => kind, retry_after_milliseconds => wait) -> breaker_refused(kind = kind, retry_after_milliseconds = wait)
        case _ -> null
      }
    }
    case _ -> null
  }
}

@"Middleware over the seam: install ONE breaker for every model call in @continuation@'s dynamic extent
— every desk, every session, every hand-rolled `take_turn` — inside the provider (the provider's `use`
first, then `use ai.with_breaker(...)`), so a refused step never reaches the provider at all.

@failure_threshold@ consecutive failed steps OPEN it; while open, `infer_step` answers
`inference_failed(breaker_refusal_error(...))` WITHOUT calling out, except for the one probe step per
interval that tests for recovery. @on_transition@ fires on the STATE CHANGE and nowhere else — once on
the way out, once on the way back — which is what makes the notice structurally un-floodable: it is
read off the state the transition just produced, so there is no count tested anywhere else and no way
for a still-closed breaker to announce. WHERE those two lines go and HOW they read is the app's data:
the sink gets the `outage_kind` (`outage_line` / `recovery_line` are the wordings this package
suggests), and an app with both an operator's channel and a public one sends the remedy to the first
and the transient wording to the second.

`infer_object` passes through un-broken: a structured extraction is a one-shot call whose caller is
right there to see it fail."
agent with_breaker[R, effect E](
  @"Where the two lines per outage go, and what they say. Called once when the breaker opens and once when it closes." on_transition: agent (event: breaker_event) -> null with {...E, infer_step},
  @"The program to run with the breaker installed." continuation: agent (value: null) -> R with {...E, infer_step},
  @"The breaker policy; `null` (the default) is `default_breaker_policy`." policy: breaker_policy | null ?= null,
) -> R with E | infer_step | io {
  let rules = match (policy) {
    case null -> default_breaker_policy()
    case given -> given
  }
  use handler (var breaker: breaker_state = model_answering(consecutive_failures = 0)) {
    request infer_step(history: array[types.message], tool_metas: array[reflection.agent_metadata]) -> step_outcome {
      // ONE clock read per step, so the probe-due test and the instant recorded on the breaker are the
      // same instant rather than two calls apart.
      let now = time.now()
      match (may_call(breaker = breaker, now = now)) {
        case hold_off(down => down) -> {
          next inference_failed(error = breaker_refusal_error(down = down, now = now)) with { breaker = breaker }
        }
        case call_now(_) -> {
          // Re-performing escalates PAST this handler to the ambient provider — the outward re-perform
          // semantics a rethrown request uses.
          let outcome = infer_step(history = history, tool_metas = tool_metas)
          match (outcome) {
            case inferred(result => _) -> {
              // The recovery notice is read off the state being LEFT, so it fires exactly once.
              match (breaker) {
                case model_down(kind => kind, next_probe => _, next_delay => _) -> on_transition(event = breaker_closed(kind = kind))
                case model_answering(consecutive_failures => _) -> null
              }
              next outcome with { breaker = model_answering(consecutive_failures = 0) }
            }
            case inference_failed(error => error) -> {
              let settled = after_failure(breaker = breaker, policy = rules, kind = classify_outage(error = error), now = now)
              // Likewise the opening notice: the transition is the pair (was closed, is now open), and
              // there is no other place a count is tested.
              match (breaker) {
                case model_answering(consecutive_failures => _) -> {
                  match (settled) {
                    case model_down(kind => opened, next_probe => _, next_delay => _) -> on_transition(event = breaker_opened(kind = opened))
                    case model_answering(consecutive_failures => _) -> null
                  }
                }
                case model_down(kind => _, next_probe => _, next_delay => _) -> null
              }
              next outcome with { breaker = settled }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  continuation(value = null)
}
